\id MAT - The Text-Critical English New Testament
\h THE GOSPEL ACCORDING TO MATTHEW
\toc1 The Gospel According to Matthew
\toc2 Matthew
\toc3 Matt.
\mt2 THE GOSPEL ACCORDING TO
\mt1 MATTHEW
\c 1
\s1 The Genealogy of Jesus Christ
\m
\v 1 The book of the genealogy of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham.
\p
\v 2 Abraham was the father of Isaac, Isaac was the father of Jacob, Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers,
\v 3 Judah was the father of Perez and Zerah by Tamar, Perez was the father of Hezron, Hezron was the father of Ram,
\v 4 Ram was the father of Amminadab, Amminadab was the father of Nahshon, Nahshon was the father of Salmon,
\v 5 Salmon was the father of Boaz by Rahab, Boaz was the father of Obed by Ruth, Obed was the father of Jesse,
\v 6 and Jesse was the father of David the king.
\p David \f + \fr 1:6 \fq the king \ft ¦ — CT\f*the king was the father of Solomon by the wife of Uriah,
\v 7 Solomon was the father of Rehoboam, Rehoboam was the father of Abijah, Abijah was the father of \f + \fr 1:7 \fq Asa, Asa \ft ¦ Asaph, Asaph CT\f*Asa,
\v 8 Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat, Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram, Joram was the father of Uzziah,
\v 9 Uzziah was the father of Jotham, Jotham was the father of Ahaz, Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah,
\v 10 Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh, Manasseh was the father of \f + \fr 1:10 \fq Amon, Amon \ft ¦ Amos, Amos CT\f*Amon, Amon was the father of Josiah,
\v 11 and Josiah was the father of Jechoniah and his brothers at the time of the Babylonian resettlement.
\p
\v 12 After the Babylonian resettlement, Jechoniah was the father of Shealtiel, Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel,
\v 13 Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud, Abiud was the father of Eliakim, Eliakim was the father of Azor,
\v 14 Azor was the father of Zadok, Zadok was the father of Achim, Achim was the father of Eliud,
\v 15 Eliud was the father of Eleazar, Eleazar was the father of Matthan, Matthan was the father of Jacob,
\v 16 and Jacob was the father of Joseph the husband of Mary, of whom was born Jesus, who is called Christ.
\p
\v 17 So in all there were fourteen generations from Abraham to David, fourteen generations from David to the Babylonian resettlement, and fourteen generations from the Babylonian resettlement to the Christ.
\s1 The Birth of Jesus
\m
\v 18 Now the birth of Jesus Christ took place in this way: When his mother Mary had been betrothed to Joseph, before they came together, she was found to be with child from the Holy Spirit.
\v 19 Because her husband Joseph was a righteous man and did not want to shame her publicly, he intended to send her away privately.
\v 20 But as he thought about these things, behold, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream and said, “Joseph, Son of David, do not be afraid to receive Mary as yoʋr wife, for the child that is conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit.
\v 21 She will give birth to a son, and yoʋ shall name him Jesus, for he will save his people from their sins.”
\v 22 All this took place to fulfill what the Lord had spoken through the prophet:
\b
\q1
\v 23 “Behold, the virgin will conceive and give birth to a son,
\q1 and they will name him Immanuel”
\b
\m (which means, “God with us”).
\v 24 When Joseph woke from his sleep, he did as the angel of the Lord had commanded him. He took his wife
\v 25 but had no marital relations with her until she had given birth to \f + \fr 1:25 \fq her firstborn \ft 98.6% ¦ a CT 0.5%\f*her firstborn son. And he named him Jesus.
\c 2
\s1 The Visit of the Wise Men
\m
\v 1 After Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea, in the days of Herod the king, behold, wise men from the east arrived in Jerusalem,
\v 2 saying, “Where is the child who has been born king of the Jews? For we saw his star in the east and have come to worship him.”
\v 3 When Herod the king heard this, he was troubled, and all Jerusalem with him.
\v 4 So he assembled all the chief priests and scribes of the people, and inquired of them where the Christ was to be born.
\v 5 They told him, “In Bethlehem of Judea, for so it is written by the prophet,
\b
\q1
\v 6 ‘But yoʋ Bethlehem, in the land of Judah,
\q1 are by no means least among the rulers of Judah;
\q1 for from yoʋ will come a ruler
\q1 who will shepherd my people Israel.’ ”
\b
\p
\v 7 Then Herod secretly called the wise men and ascertained from them what time the star had appeared.
\v 8 He sent them to Bethlehem and said, “Go search diligently for the child, and when you have found him, bring me word so that I too may go and worship him.”
\v 9 They listened to the king and went on their way, and behold, the star they had seen in the east went before them until it came and stopped over the place where the child was.
\v 10 When they saw the star, they rejoiced with exceedingly great joy.
\v 11 As they came into the house and \f + \fr 2:11 \fq saw \ft ¦ found ELZ ST\f*saw the child with his mother Mary, they fell down and worshiped him. Then they opened their treasures and offered him gifts: gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
\v 12 And being warned in a dream not to return to Herod, they departed for their own country by another way. \fig And behold, the star they had seen in the east went before them until it came and stopped over the place where the child was.|src="01 Matthew 02_9.png" size="span" copy="Public Domain (Gustave Doré)" ref="2:9" \fig*
\s1 The Escape to Egypt
\m
\v 13 After they were gone, behold, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream, saying, “Rise, take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt, and stay there until I tell yoʋ, for Herod is about to seek the child, to destroy him.”
\v 14 So Joseph rose, took the child and his mother by night, and departed for Egypt,
\v 15 where he stayed until the death of Herod. This was to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet: “Out of Egypt I called my son.”
\s1 The Massacre of the Innocent Children
\m
\v 16 When Herod saw that he had been fooled by the wise men, he was greatly enraged and sent men to kill all the boys in Bethlehem and in its vicinity who were two years old and under, according to the time that he had ascertained from the wise men.
\v 17 Then what had been spoken by the prophet Jeremiah was fulfilled:
\b
\q1
\v 18 “A voice was heard in Ramah,
\q1 \f + \fr 2:18 \fq wailing, weeping, \ft 99% ¦ weeping CT 0.7%\f*wailing, weeping, and great lamentation,
\q1 Rachel weeping for her children,
\q1 and she refused to be comforted, because they are no more.”
\s1 The Return to Nazareth
\m
\v 19 After Herod died, behold, an angel of the Lord appeared in a dream to Joseph in Egypt,
\v 20 saying, “Rise, take the child and his mother, and go to the land of Israel, for those who were seeking the child's life are dead.”
\v 21 So Joseph rose, took the child and his mother, and went to the land of Israel.
\v 22 But when he heard that Archelaus was reigning over Judea in place of his father Herod, he was afraid to go there. And being warned in a dream, he withdrew to the district of Galilee
\v 23 and came and dwelt in a town called Nazareth, so that what had been spoken by the prophets might be fulfilled: “He will be called a Nazarene.”
\c 3
\s1 The Preaching of John the Baptist
\m
\v 1 In those days John the Baptist came preaching in the wilderness of Judea, saying,
\v 2 “Repent, for the kingdom of heaven has drawn near.”
\v 3 This is the one who was spoken of by the prophet Isaiah, who said,
\b
\q1 “The voice of one crying out in the wilderness,
\q1 ‘Prepare the way for the Lord;
\q1 make his paths straight.’ ”
\b
\m
\v 4 Now John wore a garment of camel's hair and a leather belt around his waist, and his food was locusts and wild honey.
\v 5 Then Jerusalem was going out to him, and so were all Judea and all the region around the Jordan.
\v 6 They were being baptized by him in the \f + \fr 3:6 \fq Jordan \ft ¦ Jordan River CT\f*Jordan, confessing their sins.
\p
\v 7 But when John saw many of the Pharisees and Sadducees coming to his baptism, he said to them, “You brood of vipers, who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?
\v 8 Produce \f + \fr 3:8 \fq fruit \ft ¦ fruits TR\f*fruit consistent with repentance.
\v 9 And do not think that you can say to yourselves, ‘We have Abraham as our father,’ for I tell you that God is able from these stones to raise up children for Abraham.
\v 10 Even now the axe is laid to the root of the trees. Every tree therefore that does not produce good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
\p
\v 11 “I baptize you with water for repentance, but he who is coming after me is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to carry. He will baptize you with the Holy \f + \fr 3:11 \fq Spirit \ft ¦ Spirit and fire ANT CT TR\f*Spirit.
\v 12 His winnowing fork is in his hand, and he will thoroughly clean out his threshing floor and gather his wheat into the barn, but the chaff he will burn up with unquenchable fire.”
\s1 The Baptism of Jesus
\m
\v 13 Then Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan to be baptized by John.
\v 14 But John tried to prevent him, saying, “I need to be baptized by yoʋ, and yet yoʋ are coming to me?”
\v 15 But Jesus answered him, \wj “Permit it for now, for thus it is fitting for us to fulfill all righteousness.”\wj* Then John permitted him.
\v 16 After being baptized, Jesus immediately went up from the water. And behold, the heavens were opened \f + \fr 3:16 \fq to him \ft 99.3% ¦ — SBL WH 0.3%\f*to him, and he saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove and alighting on him.
\v 17 And behold, a voice from heaven said, “This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.”
\c 4
\s1 The Temptation of Jesus
\m
\v 1 Then Jesus was led up into the wilderness by the Spirit to be tempted by the devil.
\v 2 After fasting for forty days and forty nights, he was hungry.
\v 3 Then the tempter came up \f + \fr 4:3 \fq to him and said \ft ¦ and said to him CT\f*to him and said, “If yoʋ are the Son of God, command these stones to become bread.”
\v 4 But Jesus answered, \wj “It is written,\wj*
\b
\q1 \wj ‘Man shall not live by bread alone,\wj*
\q1 \wj but by every word that comes from the mouth of God.’ ”\wj*
\b
\m
\v 5 Then the devil took him to the holy city, set him on the pinnacle of the temple,
\v 6 and said to him, “If yoʋ are the Son of God, throw yoʋrself down, for it is written,
\b
\q1 ‘He will command his angels concerning yoʋ,’
\b
\m and,
\b
\q1 ‘In their hands they will lift yoʋ up,
\q1 so that yoʋ do not strike yoʋr foot against a stone.’ ”
\b
\m
\v 7 Jesus said to him, \wj “Again it is written, ‘Yoʋ shall not put the Lord yoʋr God to the test.’ ”\wj*
\v 8 Again, the devil took him to a very high mountain and showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
\v 9 And he said to him, “All these things I will give yoʋ, if yoʋ will fall down and worship me.”
\v 10 Then Jesus said to him, \wj “\wj*\f + \fr 4:10 \fq Get behind me \ft 87.6% ¦ Go away CT TR 11.7%\f*\wj Get behind me, Satan! For it is written,\wj*
\b
\q1 \wj ‘Yoʋ shall worship the Lord yoʋr God,\wj*
\q1 \wj and him only shall yoʋ serve.’ ”\wj*
\b
\m
\v 11 Then the devil left him, and behold, angels came and ministered to him.
\s1 Jesus Begins His Ministry
\m
\v 12 Now when Jesus heard that John had been put in prison, he withdrew to Galilee.
\v 13 And leaving Nazareth, he went and dwelt at Capernaum by the sea, in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali,
\v 14 so that what had been spoken by the prophet Isaiah might be fulfilled:
\b
\q1
\v 15 “Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali,
\q1 way of the sea, beyond the Jordan, Galilee of the Gentiles,
\q1
\v 16 the people sitting in darkness
\q1 have seen a great light,
\q1 and on those sitting in the region and shadow of death
\q1 light has dawned.”
\b
\p
\v 17 From that time Jesus began to preach, \wj “Repent, for the kingdom of heaven has drawn near.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Calls His First Disciples
\m
\v 18 As Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee, he saw two brothers, Simon (who is called Peter) and Andrew his brother, casting a net into the sea, for they were fishermen.
\v 19 Jesus said to them, \wj “Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men.”\wj*
\v 20 So they immediately left their nets and followed him.
\v 21 Going on from there, he saw two other brothers, James the son of Zebedee and John his brother, in the boat with their father Zebedee, mending their nets, and he called them.
\v 22 So they immediately left the boat and their father and followed him.
\s1 Jesus Ministers to Great Crowds
\m
\v 23 Then Jesus went throughout all of Galilee, teaching in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every disease and every sickness among the people.
\v 24 So the news about him went out into all of Syria, and the people brought to him all who were sick with various diseases, those suffering severe pain, the demon-possessed, epileptics, and paralytics. And he healed them.
\v 25 And large crowds followed him from Galilee, the Decapolis, Jerusalem, Judea, and from beyond the Jordan.
\c 5
\s1 The Beatitudes
\m
\v 1 When Jesus saw the crowds, he went up on the mountain. And when he sat down, his disciples came to him.
\v 2 Then he opened his mouth and taught them, saying, \fig When Jesus saw the crowds, he went up on the mountain. And when he sat down, his disciples came to him. Then he opened his mouth and taught them.|src="01 Matthew 05_1-2.png" size="span" copy="Public Domain (Gustave Doré)" ref="5:1–2" \fig*
\b
\q1
\v 3 \wj “Blessed are the poor in spirit,\wj*
\q1 \wj for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.\wj*
\q1
\v 4 \wj Blessed are those who mourn,\wj*
\q1 \wj for they will be comforted.\wj*
\q1
\v 5 \wj Blessed are the meek,\wj*
\q1 \wj for they will inherit the earth.\wj*
\q1
\v 6 \wj Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness,\wj*
\q1 \wj for they will be filled.\wj*
\q1
\v 7 \wj Blessed are the merciful,\wj*
\q1 \wj for they will receive mercy.\wj*
\q1
\v 8 \wj Blessed are the pure in heart,\wj*
\q1 \wj for they will see God.\wj*
\q1
\v 9 \wj Blessed are the peacemakers,\wj*
\q1 \wj for they will be called sons of God.\wj*
\q1
\v 10 \wj Blessed are those who are persecuted for righteousness' sake,\wj*
\q1 \wj for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.\wj*
\b
\m
\v 11 \wj Blessed are you when others reproach you, persecute you, and say all kinds of evil against you falsely on my account.\wj*
\v 12 \wj Rejoice and be glad, for great is your reward in heaven; for in the same way they persecuted the prophets who were before you.\wj*
\s1 Salt and Light
\m
\v 13 \wj “You are the salt of the earth, but if salt loses its flavor, with what shall it be seasoned? It is no longer fit for anything but to be thrown out and trampled under people's feet.\wj*
\p
\v 14 \wj “You are the light of the world. A city set on a hill cannot be hidden,\wj*
\v 15 \wj nor do people light a lamp and put it under a basket, but on a lampstand, and it shines for all who are in the house.\wj*
\v 16 \wj Let your light shine before others in such a way that they may see your good works and glorify your Father in heaven.\wj*
\s1 Christ Fulfills the Law and the Prophets
\m
\v 17 \wj “Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets. I have not come to abolish them, but to fulfill them.\wj*
\v 18 \wj For truly I say to you, until heaven and earth pass away, not one letter or stroke of a letter will by any means pass from the law until all is accomplished.\wj*
\v 19 \wj Therefore whoever does away with one of the least of these commandments and teaches others to do likewise will be called least in the kingdom of heaven, but whoever practices them and teaches them will be called great in the kingdom of heaven.\wj*
\v 20 \wj For I tell you, unless your righteousness surpasses that of the scribes and Pharisees, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven.\wj*
\s1 Anger
\m
\v 21 \wj “You have heard that it was said to those of old, ‘Yoʋ shall not murder,’ and, ‘Whoever murders will be subject to judgment.’\wj*
\v 22 \wj But I say to you that everyone who is\wj* \f + \fr 5:22 \fq rashly \ft 95.4% ¦ — CT 1.9%\f*\wj rashly angry with his brother will be subject to judgment. Whoever says to his brother, ‘Yoʋ idiot,’ will be subject to the Sanhedrin. And whoever says, ‘Yoʋ fool,’ will be subject to the fires of hell.\wj*
\v 23 \wj So if yoʋ are offering yoʋr gift at the altar and there remember that yoʋr brother has something against yoʋ,\wj*
\v 24 \wj leave yoʋr gift there before the altar and go. First be reconciled to yoʋr brother, and then come and offer yoʋr gift.\wj*
\v 25 \wj Make friends with yoʋr adversary quickly while yoʋ are on the way with him, lest yoʋr adversary hand yoʋ over to the judge, and the judge\wj* \f + \fr 5:25 \fq hand yoʋ over \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj hand yoʋ over to the guard, and yoʋ be thrown into prison.\wj*
\v 26 \wj Truly I say to yoʋ, yoʋ will certainly not come out of there until yoʋ have paid the last penny.\wj*
\s1 Adultery
\m
\v 27 \wj “You have heard that it was\wj* \f + \fr 5:27 \fq said \ft ¦ said to those of old ANT TR\f*\wj said, ‘Yoʋ shall not commit adultery.’\wj*
\v 28 \wj But I say to you that anyone who looks at a woman to lust after her has already committed adultery with her in his heart.\wj*
\v 29 \wj So if yoʋr right eye causes yoʋ to stumble, pluck it out and throw it away from yoʋ. For it is better for yoʋ to have one of yoʋr members perish, than for yoʋr whole body to be thrown into hell.\wj*
\v 30 \wj And if yoʋr right hand causes yoʋ to stumble, cut it off and throw it away from yoʋ. For it is better for yoʋ to have one of yoʋr members perish, than for yoʋr whole body to\wj* \f + \fr 5:30 \fq be thrown \ft ¦ go CT\f*\wj be thrown into hell.\wj*
\s1 Divorce
\m
\v 31 \wj “It was also said, ‘Whoever divorces his wife must give her a certificate of divorce.’\wj*
\v 32 \wj But I say to you that\wj* \f + \fr 5:32 \fq whoever \ft ¦ anyone who CT\f*\wj whoever divorces his wife, apart from a matter of fornication, makes her commit adultery. And whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery.\wj*
\s1 Oaths
\m
\v 33 \wj “Again, you have heard that it was said to those of old, ‘Yoʋ shall not break an oath, but shall keep yoʋr oaths to the Lord.’\wj*
\v 34 \wj But I say to you, do not swear at all, neither by heaven, for it is the throne of God;\wj*
\v 35 \wj nor by the earth, for it is the footstool of his feet; nor by Jerusalem, for it is the city of the great King.\wj*
\v 36 \wj Neither shall yoʋ swear by yoʋr head, for yoʋ cannot make one hair white or black.\wj*
\v 37 \wj But let your ‘Yes’ be ‘Yes’ and your ‘No’ be ‘No’; for whatever is more than these is from the evil one.\wj*
\s1 Retaliation
\m
\v 38 \wj “You have heard that it was said, ‘An eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth.’\wj*
\v 39 \wj But I say to you, do not resist one who is evil. Rather, if anyone slaps yoʋ on\wj* \f + \fr 5:39 \fq yoʋr \ft ¦ the ANT PCK RP SBL TH\f*\wj yoʋr right cheek, turn to him the other also.\wj*
\v 40 \wj If anyone wants to sue yoʋ and take away yoʋr tunic, let him have yoʋr cloak also.\wj*
\v 41 \wj And if anyone presses yoʋ into service for one mile, go with him two.\wj*
\v 42 \wj Give to everyone who asks of yoʋ, and do not turn away from anyone who wants to borrow from yoʋ.\wj*
\s1 Love for Enemies
\m
\v 43 \wj “You have heard that it was said, ‘Yoʋ shall love yoʋr neighbor and hate yoʋr enemy.’\wj*
\v 44 \wj But I say to you, love your enemies,\wj* \f + \fr 5:44 \fq bless those who curse you, do good to those who hate you, \ft 91.2% ¦ — CT 0.8%\f*\wj bless those who curse you, do good to those who hate you, and pray for those who\wj* \f + \fr 5:44 \fq mistreat you and \ft 87.6% ¦ — CT 0.8%\f*\wj mistreat you and persecute you,\wj*
\v 45 \wj so that you may be sons of your Father in heaven. For he makes his sun rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous.\wj*
\v 46 \wj For if you love those who love you, what reward do you have? Do not even the tax collectors do the same?\wj*
\v 47 \wj And if you greet only your\wj* \f + \fr 5:47 \fq friends \ft ¦ brothers CT TR\f*\wj friends, what are you doing that is out of the ordinary? Do not even the\wj* \f + \fr 5:47 \fq tax collectors do that \ft 76.6% ¦ tax collectors do the same MSS 13.2% \ft ¦ Gentiles do the same CT 3.7%\f*\wj tax collectors do that?\wj*
\v 48 \wj You therefore shall be perfect, just as your\wj* \f + \fr 5:48 \fq Father in heaven \ft ¦ heavenly Father CT\f*\wj Father in heaven is perfect.\wj*
\c 6
\s1 Giving to the Needy
\m
\v 1 \wj “Be careful not to\wj* \f + \fr 6:1 \fq give to the needy \ft ¦ practice your righteousness CT\f*\wj give to the needy in front of others to be seen by them, otherwise you have no reward from your Father in heaven.\wj*
\v 2 \wj So when yoʋ give to the needy, do not sound a trumpet before yoʋ, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and in the streets, so that they may be praised by others. Truly I say to you, they have received their reward.\wj*
\v 3 \wj But when yoʋ give to the needy, do not let yoʋr left hand know what yoʋr right hand is doing,\wj*
\v 4 \wj so that yoʋr giving may be in secret. Then yoʋr Father who sees in secret will\wj* \f + \fr 6:4 \fq himself reward yoʋ openly \ft 73.5% ¦ reward yoʋ openly ANT 18.4% ¦ reward yoʋ CT 5.5%\f*\wj himself reward yoʋ openly.\wj*
\s1 Prayer
\m
\v 5 \wj “When\wj* \f + \fr 6:5 \fq yoʋ \ft ¦ you CT\f*\wj yoʋ pray, do not be like the hypocrites. For they love to pray standing in the synagogues and at the street corners so that they may be seen by others. Truly I say to you, they have received their reward.\wj*
\v 6 \wj But when yoʋ pray, go into yoʋr inner room, shut yoʋr door, and pray to yoʋr Father who is in secret. Then yoʋr Father who sees in secret will reward yoʋ\wj* \f + \fr 6:6 \fq openly \ft 96.9% ¦ — CT 1.9%\f*\wj openly.\wj*
\p
\v 7 \wj “And when you pray, do not babble on repetitiously like the Gentiles do, for they think they will be heard because of their use of many words.\wj*
\v 8 \wj Do not be like them, for\wj* \f + \fr 6:8 \fq your \ft ¦ God your WH\f*\wj your Father knows what you need before you ask him.\wj*
\v 9 \wj Pray then like this:\wj*
\b
\q1 \wj Our Father in heaven,\wj*
\q1 \wj hallowed be yoʋr name.\wj*
\q1
\v 10 \wj Yoʋr kingdom come.\wj*
\q1 \wj Yoʋr will be done,\wj*
\q1 \wj on earth as it is in heaven.\wj*
\q1
\v 11 \wj Give us this day our daily bread.\wj*
\q1
\v 12 \wj And forgive us our debts,\wj*
\q1 \wj as we\wj* \f + \fr 6:12 \fq forgive \ft ¦ have forgiven CT\f*\wj forgive our debtors.\wj*
\q1
\v 13 \wj And lead us not into temptation,\wj*
\q1 \wj but deliver us from evil.\wj*
\q1 \f + \fr 6:13 \fq For yoʋrs is the kingdom and the power and the glory forever. Amen. \ft 92.6% ¦ — CT 1.2%\f*\wj For yoʋrs is the kingdom\wj*
\q1 \wj and the power and the glory forever. Amen.\wj*
\b
\m
\v 14 \wj For if you forgive others their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you.\wj*
\v 15 \wj But if you do not forgive others\wj* \f + \fr 6:15 \fq their trespasses \ft 97.3% ¦ — NA SBL 1.1%\f*\wj their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses.\wj*
\s1 Fasting
\m
\v 16 \wj “When you fast, do not be gloomy like the hypocrites, for they disfigure their faces so that others will see that they are fasting. Truly I say to you, they have received their reward.\wj*
\v 17 \wj But when yoʋ fast, anoint yoʋr head and wash yoʋr face,\wj*
\v 18 \wj so that no one will see that yoʋ are fasting except yoʋr Father who is in secret. Then yoʋr Father who sees in secret will reward\wj* \f + \fr 6:18 \fq yoʋ \ft ¦ yoʋ openly ANT TR\f*\wj yoʋ.\wj*
\s1 Store Up Treasures in Heaven
\m
\v 19 \wj “Do not store up treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.\wj*
\v 20 \wj But store up treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys, and where thieves do not break in and steal.\wj*
\v 21 \wj For where\wj* \f + \fr 6:21 \fq your … your \ft ¦ yoʋr … yoʋr CT\f*\wj your treasure is, there will your heart be also.\wj*
\s1 The Lamp of the Body
\m
\v 22 \wj “The lamp of the body is the eye. If then yoʋr eye is clear, yoʋr whole body will be full of light.\wj*
\v 23 \wj But if yoʋr eye is bad, yoʋr whole body will be full of darkness. If then the light within yoʋ is darkness, how great is the darkness!\wj*
\s1 You Cannot Serve Two Masters
\m
\v 24 \wj “No one can serve two masters, for either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be devoted to one and despise the other. You cannot serve God and money.\wj*
\s1 Anxiety
\m
\v 25 \wj “Therefore I tell you, do not be anxious about your life, what you will eat\wj* \f + \fr 6:25 \fq and what you will drink \ft ¦ or what you will drink NA TH WH ¦ — SBL\f*\wj and what you will drink, or about your body, what you will wear. Is not life more than food and the body more than clothing?\wj*
\v 26 \wj Look at the birds of the sky: They do not sow or reap or gather into barns, yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not of more value than they?\wj*
\v 27 \wj Which of you by being anxious can add a single moment to yoʋr span of life?\wj*
\v 28 \wj And why are you anxious about clothing? Consider the lilies of the field, how they grow: They do not toil or spin,\wj*
\v 29 \wj yet I tell you that not even Solomon in all his glory was clothed like one of these.\wj*
\v 30 \wj Now if God so clothes the grass of the field, which is alive today and tomorrow is thrown into the oven, will he not much more clothe you, O you of little faith?\wj*
\v 31 \wj Therefore do not be anxious, saying, ‘What will we eat?’ or, ‘What will we drink?’ or, ‘What will we wear?’\wj*
\v 32 \wj For the Gentiles seek after all these things, and your heavenly Father knows that you need them all.\wj*
\v 33 \wj But seek first the kingdom\wj* \f + \fr 6:33 \fq of God \ft 97% ¦ — SBL WH 0.1%\f*\wj of God and his righteousness, and all these things will be added to you.\wj*
\p
\v 34 \wj “Therefore do not be anxious about tomorrow, for tomorrow will be anxious about\wj* \f + \fr 6:34 \fq its own matters \ft ¦ itself CT\f*\wj its own matters. Sufficient for the day is its own trouble.\wj*
\c 7
\s1 Judging Others
\m
\v 1 \wj “Do not judge, so that you will not be judged.\wj*
\v 2 \wj For with the judgment you pronounce you will be judged, and with the measure you use it will be measured to\wj* \f + \fr 7:2 \fq you \ft ¦ you in return TR\f*\wj you.\wj*
\v 3 \wj Why do yoʋ see the speck in yoʋr brother's eye, but do not consider the beam in yoʋr own eye?\wj*
\v 4 \wj Or how will yoʋ say to yoʋr brother, ‘Let me take the speck out of yoʋr eye,’ when there is a beam in yoʋr own eye?\wj*
\v 5 \wj Hypocrite! First take the beam out of yoʋr own eye, and then yoʋ will see clearly to take the speck out of yoʋr brother's eye.\wj*
\p
\v 6 \wj “Do not give what is holy to dogs, and do not throw your pearls before pigs, lest they trample them with their feet, and then turn and tear you to pieces.\wj*
\s1 Ask, Seek, Knock
\m
\v 7 \wj “Ask, and it will be given to you; seek, and you will find; knock, and the door will be opened for you.\wj*
\v 8 \wj For everyone who asks receives, and he who seeks finds, and for him who knocks the door will be opened.\wj*
\v 9 \wj Or what man is there among you, who, if his son asks him for bread, will give him a stone;\wj*
\v 10 \f + \fr 7:10 \fq and \ft ¦ or CT\f*\wj and if he asks for a fish, will give him a serpent?\wj*
\v 11 \wj If you then, being evil, know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more will your Father in heaven give good things to those who ask him!\wj*
\p
\v 12 \wj “Therefore whatever you wish that others would do to you, do the same also to them, for this is the Law and the Prophets.\wj*
\s1 The Narrow Gate
\m
\v 13 \wj “Enter through the narrow gate, for wide\wj* \f + \fr 7:13 \fq is the gate, \ft ¦ — WH\f*\wj is the gate, and broad is the way that leads to destruction, and there are many who enter through it.\wj*
\v 14 \f + \fr 7:14 \fq How … it! \ft ¦ For … it. SBL TR WH\f*\wj How narrow is the gate and restricted the way that leads to life, and there are few who find it!\wj*
\s1 Beware of False Prophets
\m
\v 15 \wj “Beware of false prophets, who come to you in sheep's clothing but inwardly are ravenous wolves.\wj*
\v 16 \wj By their fruits you will know them. Do people gather grapes from thorns, or figs from thistles?\wj*
\v 17 \wj In the same way, every good tree produces good fruit, but a bad tree produces bad fruit.\wj*
\v 18 \wj A good tree cannot produce bad fruit, neither can a bad tree produce good fruit.\wj*
\v 19 \wj Every\wj* \f + \fr 7:19 \fq tree \ft ¦ tree then PCK\f*\wj tree that does not produce good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.\wj*
\v 20 \wj Therefore by their fruits you will know them.\wj*
\s1 I Never Knew You
\m
\v 21 \wj “Not everyone who says to me, ‘Lord, Lord,’ will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only he who does the will of my Father in heaven.\wj*
\v 22 \wj Many will say to me on that day, ‘Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in yoʋr name, and in yoʋr name cast out demons, and in yoʋr name do many miracles?’\wj*
\v 23 \wj Then I will declare to them, ‘I never knew you; depart from me, you who practice lawlessness.’\wj*
\s1 The Two Foundations
\m
\v 24 \wj “Everyone then who hears these words of mine and does them\wj* \f + \fr 7:24 \fq I will compare \ft ¦ will be compared CT\f*\wj I will compare to a wise man who built his house on the rock.\wj*
\v 25 \wj The rain fell, the floods came, and the winds blew and beat on that house, but it did not fall, for it was founded on the rock.\wj*
\v 26 \wj But everyone who hears these words of mine and does not do them will be compared to a foolish man who built his house on the sand.\wj*
\v 27 \wj The rain fell, the floods came, and the winds blew and beat against that house, and it fell, and great was its fall.”\wj*
\p
\v 28 When Jesus finished saying these things, the crowds were astonished at his teaching,
\v 29 because he was teaching them as one who had authority, and not as \f + \fr 7:29 \fq the \ft ¦ their CT\f*the scribes.
\c 8
\s1 Jesus Cleanses a Man with Leprosy
\m
\v 1 When Jesus came down from the mountain, large crowds followed him.
\v 2 And behold, a leper came and bowed down before him, saying, “Lord, if yoʋ are willing, yoʋ can make me clean.”
\v 3 So Jesus stretched out his hand and touched him, saying, \wj “I am willing; be made clean.”\wj* Immediately his leprosy was cleansed.
\v 4 Then Jesus said to him, \wj “Be sure not to tell anyone about this, but go show yoʋrself to the priest, and offer the gift that Moses commanded, as a testimony to them.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Heals a Centurion's Servant
\m
\v 5 When Jesus entered Capernaum, a centurion came to him and pleaded with him,
\v 6 saying, “Lord, my servant is lying paralyzed at home, suffering terribly.”
\v 7 Jesus said to him, \wj “I will come and heal him.”\wj*
\v 8 But the centurion replied, “Lord, I am not worthy to have yoʋ enter under my roof, but just say the word, and my servant will be healed.
\v 9 For I too am a man \f + \fr 8:9 \fq under \ft 97.5% ¦ set under WH 0.7%\f*under authority, with soldiers under me. I say to this one, ‘Go,’ and he goes; and to another, ‘Come,’ and he comes; and to my servant, ‘Do this,’ and he does it.”
\v 10 When Jesus heard this, he was amazed and said to those who were following him, \wj “Truly I say to you,\wj* \f + \fr 8:10 \fq not even \ft 93.6% ¦ with no one CT 0.4%\f*\wj not even in Israel have I found such great faith.\wj*
\v 11 \wj I tell you that many will come from east and west, and recline with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob at the banquet table in the kingdom of heaven,\wj*
\v 12 \wj but the sons of the kingdom will be cast into the outer darkness; in that place there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.”\wj*
\v 13 Then Jesus said to the centurion, \wj “Go; it will be done for you just as yoʋ have believed.”\wj* And \f + \fr 8:13 \fq his \ft ¦ the SBL TH WH\f*his servant was healed in that hour.
\s1 Jesus Heals Many
\m
\v 14 When Jesus came into Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law lying in bed with a fever.
\v 15 So he touched her hand, and the fever left her. Then she rose and began to serve \f + \fr 8:15 \fq him \ft ¦ them TR\f*him.
\v 16 When evening came, many who were possessed by demons were brought to him, and he cast out the spirits with a word and healed all who were sick.
\v 17 This was to fulfill what had been spoken by the prophet Isaiah: “He took our infirmities and bore our diseases.”
\s1 Would-Be Followers of Jesus
\m
\v 18 When Jesus saw the \f + \fr 8:18 \fq large crowds \ft ¦ crowd NA WH\f*large crowds around him, he gave orders to depart to the other side of the sea.
\v 19 Then a scribe came up and said to him, “Teacher, I will follow yoʋ wherever yoʋ go.”
\v 20 Jesus said to him, \wj “Foxes have holes, and the birds of the sky have nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head.”\wj*
\v 21 Then another of \f + \fr 8:21 \fq his \ft ¦ the PCK SBL TH WH\f*his disciples said to him, “Lord, let me first go and bury my father.”
\v 22 But Jesus said to him, \wj “Follow me, and let the dead bury their own dead.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Calms a Storm
\m
\v 23 Then he got into \f + \fr 8:23 \fq the \ft ¦ a SBL TH WH\f*the boat, and his disciples followed him.
\v 24 And behold, a great storm arose on the sea, so that the boat was being swamped by the waves. But Jesus was asleep.
\v 25 So \f + \fr 8:25 \fq the disciples \ft 79.6% ¦ his disciples ANT TR 19.4% ¦ they CT 0.5%\f*the disciples came to him and woke him, saying, “Lord, save us! We are perishing!”
\v 26 But he said to them, \wj “Why are you afraid, O you of little faith?”\wj* Then he rose and rebuked the winds and the sea, and there was a great calm.
\v 27 And the men were amazed, saying, “What kind of man is this, that even the winds and the sea obey him?”
\s1 Jesus Heals Two Demon-Possessed Men
\m
\v 28 When Jesus came to the other side of the sea, to the region of the \f + \fr 8:28 \fq Gergesenes \ft ¦ Gadarenes CT\f*Gergesenes, he was met by two demon-possessed men who had come out of the tombs. They were so fierce that no one could pass by that way.
\v 29 And behold, they cried out, “What do yoʋ have to do with us, \f + \fr 8:29 \fq Jesus, \ft ¦ — CT\f*Jesus, Son of God? Have yoʋ come here to torment us before the appointed time?”
\v 30 Now at a distance from them there was a herd of many pigs feeding.
\v 31 So the demons begged Jesus, “If yoʋ cast us out, \f + \fr 8:31 \fq let us go \ft ¦ send us CT\f*let us go into the herd of pigs.”
\v 32 He said to them, \wj “Go.”\wj* So they came out and went into the \f + \fr 8:32 \fq herd of \ft ¦ — CT\f*herd of pigs, and behold, the whole herd \f + \fr 8:32 \fq of pigs \ft ¦ — CT\f*of pigs rushed down the steep bank into the sea and died in the water.
\v 33 Those who had been feeding the pigs then ran off, went into the city, and reported everything, including what had happened to the demon-possessed men.
\v 34 And behold, the whole city went out to meet Jesus. And when they saw him, they begged him to depart from their region.
\c 9
\s1 Jesus Heals a Paralytic
\m
\v 1 After getting into \f + \fr 9:1 \fq the \ft ¦ a ANT CT\f*the boat, Jesus went back across the sea and came to his own city.
\v 2 And behold, some men brought to him a paralytic lying on a bed. When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralytic, \wj “Take courage, son; yoʋr sins are forgiven\wj* \f + \fr 9:2 \fq yoʋ \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj yoʋ.”\wj*
\v 3 And behold, some of the scribes said to themselves, “This man is blaspheming.”
\v 4 \f + \fr 9:4 \fq Perceiving \ft 64% ¦ Knowing PCK SBL TH WH 32.7%\f*Perceiving their thoughts, Jesus said, \wj “Why are you thinking evil things in your hearts?\wj*
\v 5 \wj Which is easier, to say, ‘Yoʋr sins are\wj* \f + \fr 9:5 \fq forgiven \ft ¦ forgiven yoʋ TR\f*\wj forgiven,’ or to say, ‘Rise and walk’?\wj*
\v 6 \wj But so that you may know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins”\wj*—then he said to the paralytic—\wj “Rise, pick up yoʋr bed, and go to yoʋr house.”\wj*
\v 7 So the man rose and went to his house.
\v 8 When the crowds saw it, they were \f + \fr 9:8 \fq amazed \ft 97.5% ¦ afraid CT 2%\f*amazed and glorified God, who had given such authority to men.
\s1 Jesus Calls Matthew
\m
\v 9 As Jesus went on from there, he saw a man named Matthew sitting at the tax booth. Jesus said to him, \wj “Follow me,”\wj* so Matthew rose and followed him.
\p
\v 10 Later, as Jesus reclined at the table in Matthew's house, behold, many tax collectors and sinners came and reclined with him and his disciples.
\v 11 When the Pharisees saw this, they said to his disciples, “Why is your teacher \f + \fr 9:11 \fq eating \ft ¦ eating and drinking PCK\f*eating with tax collectors and sinners?”
\v 12 When Jesus heard this, he said \f + \fr 9:12 \fq to them \ft ¦ — CT\f*to them, \wj “It is not the healthy who need a physician, but the sick.\wj*
\v 13 \wj Go and learn what this means: ‘I desire mercy and not sacrifice,’ for I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners\wj* \f + \fr 9:13 \fq to repentance \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj to repentance.”\wj*
\s1 A Question About Fasting
\m
\v 14 Then the disciples of John came to Jesus and said, “Why is it that we and the Pharisees fast \f + \fr 9:14 \fq often \ft 98.3% ¦ — WH 1.4%\f*often, but yoʋr disciples do not fast?”
\v 15 Jesus said to them, \wj “Can the bridegroom's attendants mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? But the days are coming when the bridegroom will be taken away from them, and then they will fast.\wj*
\v 16 \wj No one puts a piece of unshrunk cloth on an old garment, for the patch would pull away from the garment, and a worse tear would be made.\wj*
\v 17 \wj Neither do people put new wine into old wineskins. For the wineskins would burst, spilling the wine and ruining the wineskins. But new wine is put into new wineskins, and both are preserved.”\wj*
\s1 A Girl Restored to Life and a Woman Healed
\m
\v 18 As he said these things to them, behold, a ruler came and bowed down before him, saying, “My daughter has just now died, but come and lay yoʋr hand on her, and she will live.”
\v 19 So Jesus rose and followed him, and so did his disciples.
\v 20 And behold, a woman who had suffered from a flow of blood for twelve years came up behind him and touched the fringe of his garment.
\v 21 For she said to herself, “If only I touch his garment, I will be healed.”
\v 22 But when Jesus turned \f + \fr 9:22 \fq around \ft ¦ — CT\f*around and saw her, he said, \wj “Take courage, daughter; yoʋr faith has healed yoʋ.”\wj* And the woman was healed from that hour.
\v 23 When Jesus came to the house of the ruler and saw the flute players and the crowd making a commotion,
\v 24 he said \f + \fr 9:24 \fq to them \ft ¦ — CT\f*to them, \wj “Go away, for the girl is not dead but sleeping.”\wj* And they began laughing at him.
\v 25 But when the crowd had been put outside, he went in and took hold of the girl's hand, and she arose.
\v 26 And news of this spread throughout that entire region.
\s1 Jesus Heals Two Blind Men
\m
\v 27 As Jesus went on from there, two blind men followed \f + \fr 9:27 \fq him \ft ¦ — WH\f*him, crying out, “Have mercy on us, Son of David!”
\v 28 When he went into the house, the blind men came to him, and Jesus said to them, \wj “Do you believe that I am able to do this?”\wj* They said to him, “Yes, Lord.”
\v 29 Then he touched their eyes, saying, \wj “Let it be done for yoʋ according to your faith.”\wj*
\v 30 And their eyes were opened. Then Jesus sternly warned them, \wj “Make sure no one finds out about this.”\wj*
\v 31 But they went out and spread the news about him throughout that entire region.
\s1 Jesus Heals a Mute Man
\m
\v 32 As they were going away, behold, a mute man possessed by a demon was brought to Jesus.
\v 33 When the demon had been cast out, the mute man spoke, and the crowds were amazed, saying, “Such a thing has never been seen in Israel.”
\v 34 But the Pharisees said, “By the ruler of demons he casts out demons.”
\s1 The Compassion of Jesus
\m
\v 35 Then Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every disease and every sickness \f + \fr 9:35 \fq among the people \ft ¦ — CT\f*among the people.
\v 36 When he saw the crowds, he was moved with compassion for them, because they were \f + \fr 9:36 \fq harassed \ft ¦ faint ANT TR\f*harassed and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
\v 37 Then he said to his disciples, \wj “The harvest is plentiful, but the workers are few.\wj*
\v 38 \wj Therefore ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest.”\wj* \fig Then Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every disease and every sickness among the people.|src="01 Matthew 09_35 (Heinrich Hofmann).png" size="span" copy="Public Domain (Heinrich Hofmann)" ref="9:35" \fig*
\c 10
\s1 The Twelve Apostles
\m
\v 1 Then Jesus summoned his twelve disciples and gave them authority to cast out unclean spirits and to heal every disease and every sickness.
\v 2 Now these are the names of the twelve apostles: first, Simon, who is called Peter, and Andrew his brother; James the son of Zebedee, and John his brother;
\v 3 Philip and Bartholomew; Thomas and Matthew the tax collector; James the son of Alphaeus, and \f + \fr 10:3 \fq Lebbaeus, who was given the name \ft 97.6% ¦ — CT 0.4%\f*Lebbaeus, who was given the name Thaddaeus;
\v 4 Simon the \f + \fr 10:4 \fq Cananite \ft ¦ Cananaean CT\f*Cananite, and Judas Iscariot, who betrayed him.
\s1 Jesus Sends Out the Twelve Apostles
\m
\v 5 These twelve Jesus sent out, instructing them, \wj “Do not go anywhere among the Gentiles, and do not enter any town of the Samaritans,\wj*
\v 6 \wj but go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.\wj*
\v 7 \wj As you go, preach that the kingdom of heaven has drawn near.\wj*
\v 8 \wj Heal the sick,\wj* \f + \fr 10:8 \fq cleanse lepers, \ft ¦ cleanse lepers, raise the dead, ANT TR ¦ raise the dead, cleanse lepers, CT\f*\wj cleanse lepers, and cast out demons. Freely you have received; freely give.\wj*
\v 9 \wj Take no gold, silver, or copper in your belts.\wj*
\v 10 \wj Take no knapsack for your journey, no extra tunic, sandals, or\wj* \f + \fr 10:10 \fq staffs \ft ¦ staff ANT CT ELZ ST\f*\wj staffs, for the worker is worthy of his food.\wj*
\v 11 \wj Whenever you enter a town or village, inquire who in it is worthy, and stay there until you leave that place.\wj*
\v 12 \wj As you enter the house, greet the people who live\wj* \f + \fr 10:12 \fq there. \ft ¦ there, saying, ‘May peace be upon this house!’ ANT\f*\wj there.\wj*
\v 13 \wj If the household is worthy, let your peace come upon it; but if it is not worthy, let your peace return to you.\wj*
\v 14 \wj If anyone will not receive you or listen to your words, shake off the dust from your feet as you go out from that house or town.\wj*
\v 15 \wj Truly I say to you, it will be more tolerable on the day of judgment for the land of Sodom and Gomorrah than for that town.\wj*
\s1 Persecutions Will Come
\m
\v 16 \wj “Behold, I am sending you out like sheep in the midst of wolves, so be as wise as serpents and as innocent as doves.\wj*
\v 17 \wj But beware of men, for they will deliver you up to councils and flog you in their synagogues.\wj*
\v 18 \wj For my sake you will even be brought before governors and kings as witnesses to them and to the Gentiles.\wj*
\v 19 \wj When they hand you over, do not be anxious about how to speak or what to say, for what you are to say will be given to you in that hour.\wj*
\v 20 \wj For it will not be you speaking, but the Spirit of your Father speaking through you.\wj*
\v 21 \wj Brother will deliver up brother to death, and a father his child, and children will rise up against their parents and have them put to death.\wj*
\v 22 \wj You will be hated by all because of my name, but he who endures to the end will be saved.\wj*
\v 23 \wj When they persecute you in one town, flee to another, for truly I say to you, you will certainly not finish going through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes.\wj*
\p
\v 24 \wj “A disciple is not above his teacher, nor is a servant above his master.\wj*
\v 25 \wj It is sufficient for a disciple to be like his teacher and for a servant to be like his master. If they have\wj* \f + \fr 10:25 \fq called the master of the house \ft ¦ stigmatized the master of the house as PCK\f*\wj called the master of the house\wj* \f + \fr 10:25 \fq Beelzebul \ft ¦ Beelzebub SCR\f*\wj Beelzebul, how much more will they malign the members of his household!\wj*
\s1 Fear God Alone
\m
\v 26 \wj “So do not be afraid of them, for there is nothing covered that will not be revealed, and nothing hidden that will not be made known.\wj*
\v 27 \wj What I tell you in the darkness, speak in the light; and what you hear whispered in your ear, proclaim on the housetops.\wj*
\v 28 \wj Do not be afraid of those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul, but rather fear him who can destroy both the soul and the body in hell.\wj*
\v 29 \wj Are not two sparrows sold for a penny? Yet not one of them will fall to the ground without your Father's consent.\wj*
\v 30 \wj And even the hairs of your head are all numbered.\wj*
\v 31 \wj So do not be afraid; you are of more value than many sparrows.\wj*
\s1 Acknowledging Christ
\m
\v 32 \wj “Therefore everyone who acknowledges me before men, I will also acknowledge before my Father in heaven.\wj*
\v 33 \wj But whoever denies me before men, I will also deny before my Father in heaven.\wj*
\s1 Not Peace, but a Sword
\m
\v 34 \wj “Do not think that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I have not come to bring peace, but a sword.\wj*
\v 35 \wj For I have come to set a man against his father, a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law.\wj*
\v 36 \wj And a man's enemies will be the members of his own household.\wj*
\v 37 \wj Whoever loves his father or mother more than me is not worthy of me; whoever loves his son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.\wj*
\v 38 \wj And whoever does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.\wj*
\v 39 \wj Whoever finds his life will lose it, but whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.\wj*
\s1 Rewards
\m
\v 40 \wj “Whoever receives you receives me, and whoever receives me receives him who sent me.\wj*
\v 41 \wj Whoever receives a prophet in the name of a prophet will receive a prophet's reward; whoever receives a righteous person in the name of a righteous person will receive a righteous person's reward.\wj*
\v 42 \wj And whoever gives one of these little ones even a cup of cold water to drink in the name of a disciple, truly I say to you, he will certainly not lose his reward.”\wj*
\c 11
\m
\v 1 When Jesus had finished instructing his twelve disciples, he departed from there to teach and preach in the cities of Galilee.
\s1 Messengers from John the Baptist
\m
\v 2 Now when John heard in prison about the works Christ had done, he sent \f + \fr 11:2 \fq two of \ft ¦ — CT\f*two of his disciples
\v 3 to ask him, “Are yoʋ the one who is to come, or should we wait for another?”
\v 4 Jesus answered them, \wj “Go tell John what you hear and see:\wj*
\v 5 \wj The blind receive their sight and the lame walk, lepers are made clean and the deaf hear, the dead are raised and the poor have good news preached to them.\wj*
\v 6 \wj And blessed is anyone who does not take offense at me.”\wj*
\p
\v 7 As these men went away, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John: \wj “What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?\wj*
\v 8 \wj What then did you go out to see? A man clothed in soft garments? Behold, those who wear soft garments are in the palaces of kings.\wj*
\v 9 \wj But\wj* \f + \fr 11:9 \fq what did you go out to see? A prophet? \ft ¦ why did you go out? To see a prophet? SBL WH\f*\wj what did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.\wj*
\v 10 \f + \fr 11:10 \fq For this \ft ¦ This CT\f*\wj For this is the one of whom it is written,\wj*
\b
\q1 \wj ‘Behold, I am sending my messenger ahead of yoʋ,\wj*
\q1 \wj who will prepare yoʋr way before yoʋ.’\wj*
\b
\m
\v 11 \wj Truly I say to you, among those born of women there has arisen no one greater than John the Baptist, but whoever is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.\wj*
\v 12 \wj From the days of John the Baptist until now the kingdom of heaven has been suffering violence, and the violent try to take it by force.\wj*
\v 13 \wj For all the prophets and the law prophesied until John.\wj*
\v 14 \wj And if you are willing to accept it, he is Elijah who is to come.\wj*
\v 15 \wj He who has ears\wj* \f + \fr 11:15 \fq to hear \ft ¦ — NA SBL WH\f*\wj to hear, let him hear.\wj*
\p
\v 16 \wj “But to what should I compare this generation? It is like\wj* \f + \fr 11:16 \fq children \ft ¦ little children TR\f*\wj children sitting in the\wj* \f + \fr 11:16 \fq marketplaces \ft ¦ marketplace PCK\f*\wj marketplaces and calling out to their companions,\wj*
\b
\q1
\v 17 \wj ‘We played the flute for you, but you did not dance;\wj*
\q1 \wj we sang a lament\wj* \f + \fr 11:17 \fq for you \ft 97.4% ¦ — CT 1.4%\f*\wj for you, but you did not mourn.’\wj*
\b
\m
\v 18 \wj For John came neither eating nor drinking, and they say, ‘He has a demon.’\wj*
\v 19 \wj The Son of Man came eating and drinking, and they say, ‘Behold, a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners!’ Yet wisdom is vindicated by her\wj* \f + \fr 11:19 \fq children \ft 97.7% ¦ works CT 0.4%\f*\wj children.”\wj*
\s1 Woe to the Unrepentant
\m
\v 20 Then Jesus began to reproach the cities in which most of his miracles had been done, because they did not repent:
\v 21 \wj “Woe to yoʋ, Chorazin! Woe to yoʋ, Bethsaida! For if the miracles that were done in you had been done in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long\wj* \f + \fr 11:21 \fq ago \ft ¦ ago, sitting ANT\f*\wj ago in sackcloth and ashes.\wj*
\v 22 \wj But I tell you, it will be more tolerable on the day of judgment for Tyre and Sidon than for you.\wj*
\v 23 \wj And yoʋ, Capernaum,\wj* \f + \fr 11:23 \fq who have been exalted to heaven, \ft ¦ will yoʋ be exalted to heaven? No, yoʋ CT\f*\wj who have been exalted to heaven, will\wj* \f + \fr 11:23 \fq be brought \ft 97.8% ¦ go NA SBL WH 0.4%\f*\wj be brought down to Hades. For if the miracles that were done in yoʋ had been done in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.\wj*
\v 24 \wj But I tell you, it will be more tolerable on the day of judgment for the land of Sodom than for yoʋ.”\wj*
\s1 Come to Me, and I Will Give You Rest
\m
\v 25 At that time Jesus said, \wj “I praise yoʋ, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because yoʋ have hidden these things from the wise and intelligent, and revealed them to infants.\wj*
\v 26 \wj Yes, Father, for so it was well-pleasing in yoʋr sight.\wj*
\v 27 \wj All things have been delivered to me by my Father. No one knows the Son except the Father, and no one knows the Father except the Son, and anyone to whom the Son chooses to reveal him.\wj*
\v 28 \wj Come to me, all of you who labor and are burdened, and I will give you rest.\wj*
\v 29 \wj Take my yoke upon you and learn from me, for I am gentle and lowly in heart, and you will find rest for your souls.\wj*
\v 30 \wj For my yoke is easy, and my burden is light.”\wj*
\c 12
\s1 Jesus Is Lord of the Sabbath
\m
\v 1 At that time Jesus went through the grainfields on the Sabbath. His disciples were hungry and began to pluck heads of grain and eat them.
\v 2 When the Pharisees saw this, they said to him, “Behold, yoʋr disciples are doing what is not lawful to do on the Sabbath.”
\v 3 He said to them, \wj “Have you not read what David did when he was hungry,\wj* \f + \fr 12:3 \fq and \ft ¦ he and ANT HF PCK RP TR\f*\wj and those who were with him,\wj*
\v 4 \wj how he entered the house of God and\wj* \f + \fr 12:4 \fq ate \ft ¦ they ate NA SBL WH\f*\wj ate the bread of the Presence, which was not lawful for him or those who were with him to eat, but only for the priests?\wj*
\v 5 \wj Or have you not read in the law that on the Sabbath the priests in the temple profane the Sabbath and yet are guiltless?\wj*
\v 6 \wj But I tell you that\wj* \f + \fr 12:6 \fq something \ft ¦ someone TR\f*\wj something greater than the temple is here.\wj*
\v 7 \wj But if you had known what this means: ‘I desire mercy and not sacrifice,’ you would not have condemned the guiltless.\wj*
\v 8 \wj For the Son of Man is Lord\wj* \f + \fr 12:8 \fq of \ft ¦ even of ANT TR\f*\wj of the Sabbath.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Heals a Man with a Withered Hand
\m
\v 9 Then he departed from there and went into their synagogue.
\v 10 And behold, there was a \f + \fr 12:10 \fq man \ft ¦ man there ANT\f*man who had a withered hand. In order to accuse Jesus, the Pharisees asked him, “Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?”
\v 11 He said to them, \wj “What man among you who has a sheep that falls into a pit on the Sabbath will not take hold of it and lift it out?\wj*
\v 12 \wj Of how much more value is a person than a sheep! So then, it is lawful to do good on the Sabbath.”\wj*
\v 13 Then he said to the man, \wj “Stretch out yoʋr hand.”\wj* So he stretched it out, and it was restored, becoming as sound as the other.
\v 14 But the Pharisees went out and took counsel against Jesus as to how they might destroy him.
\s1 God's Chosen Servant
\m
\v 15 Aware of this, Jesus withdrew from there. \f + \fr 12:15 \fq Large crowds \ft 97.1% ¦ Many SBL WH 0.3%\f*Large crowds followed him, and he healed them all
\v 16 and warned them not to make him known.
\v 17 This was to fulfill what had been spoken through the prophet Isaiah:
\b
\q1
\v 18 “Behold, my servant whom I have chosen,
\q1 my beloved, in whom my soul is well pleased.
\q1 I will put my Spirit upon him,
\q1 and he will declare justice to the Gentiles.
\q1
\v 19 He will not quarrel or cry out,
\q1 nor will anyone hear his voice in the streets.
\q1
\v 20 A bruised reed he will not break,
\q1 and a smoldering wick he will not extinguish,
\q1 until he brings justice to victory.
\q1
\v 21 And in his name the Gentiles will hope.”
\s1 Jesus and Beelzebul
\m
\v 22 Then a demon-possessed man who was blind and mute was brought to him, and Jesus healed him, so that the \f + \fr 12:22 \fq man who was blind and mute \ft ¦ mute man CT\f*man who was blind and mute could speak and see.
\v 23 All the crowds were amazed and said, “Can this be the \f + \fr 12:23 \fq Son \ft ¦ Christ, the Son ANT PCK\f*Son of David?”
\v 24 But when the Pharisees heard this, they said, “It is only by Beelzebul, the ruler of demons, that this man casts out demons.”
\v 25 Knowing their thoughts, Jesus said to them, \wj “Every kingdom divided against itself is laid waste, and no city or house divided against itself will stand.\wj*
\v 26 \wj So if Satan casts out Satan, he is divided against himself. How then will his kingdom stand?\wj*
\v 27 \wj And if I cast out demons by Beelzebul, by whom\wj* \f + \fr 12:27 \fq do \ft ¦ will ANT\f*\wj do your followers cast them out? Therefore they will be your judges.\wj*
\v 28 \wj But if I cast out demons by the Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.\wj*
\v 29 \wj Or how can anyone enter the house of a strong man and plunder his goods unless he first binds the strong man? Then he\wj* \f + \fr 12:29 \fq can \ft ¦ will ANT CT HF RP TR\f*\wj can plunder his house.\wj*
\v 30 \wj Whoever is not with me is against me, and whoever does not gather with me scatters.\wj*
\v 31 \wj Therefore I tell you, people will be forgiven for every sin and blasphemy, but\wj* \f + \fr 12:31 \fq they will not be forgiven for blasphemy against the Spirit \ft ¦ blasphemy against the Spirit will not be forgiven CT\f*\wj they will not be forgiven for blasphemy against the Spirit.\wj*
\v 32 \wj Whoever speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, neither in\wj* \f + \fr 12:32 \fq the present \ft ¦ this CT TR\f*\wj the present age nor in the age to come.\wj* \fig Then a demon-possessed man who was blind and mute was brought to him, and Jesus healed him, so that the man who was blind and mute could speak and see.|src="01 Matthew 12_22.png" size="span" copy="Public Domain (Gustave Doré)" ref="12:22" \fig*
\s1 A Tree Is Known by Its Fruit
\m
\v 33 \wj “A tree is known by its fruit. If a tree is good, its fruit will also be good. If a tree is bad, its fruit will also be bad.\wj*
\v 34 \wj You brood of vipers, how can you speak good things, when you are evil? For out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks.\wj*
\v 35 \wj The good person brings good things out of\wj* \f + \fr 12:35 \fq his good treasure \ft ¦ the good treasure of his heart TR\f*\wj his good treasure, and the evil person brings evil things out of his evil treasure.\wj*
\v 36 \wj But I tell you, on the day of judgment people will give an account for every idle word they speak.\wj*
\v 37 \wj For by yoʋr words yoʋ will be justified, and by yoʋr words yoʋ will be condemned.”\wj*
\s1 The Sign of Jonah
\m
\v 38 Then some of the scribes and Pharisees \f + \fr 12:38 \fq responded \ft ¦ responded to him CT\f*responded, “Teacher, we want to see a sign from yoʋ.”
\v 39 But Jesus answered them, \wj “An evil and adulterous generation asks for a sign, but no sign will be given to it except the sign of the prophet Jonah.\wj*
\v 40 \wj For just as\wj* \f + \fr 12:40 \fq Jonah \ft ¦ the prophet Jonah ANT\f*\wj Jonah was in the belly of the great sea creature for three days and three nights, so will the Son of Man be in the heart of the earth for three days and three nights.\wj*
\v 41 \wj The men of Nineveh will rise up at the judgment with this generation and condemn it, for they repented at the preaching of Jonah, and behold, something greater than Jonah is here.\wj*
\v 42 \wj The queen of the South will rise up at the judgment with this generation and condemn it, for she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon, and behold, something greater than Solomon is here.\wj*
\s1 An Unclean Spirit Returns
\m
\v 43 \wj “When an unclean spirit has gone out of a person, it goes through waterless places seeking rest, but does not find any.\wj*
\v 44 \wj Then it says, ‘I will return to the house I left.’ When it arrives, it finds the house unoccupied, swept, and put in order.\wj*
\v 45 \wj Then it goes and takes with it seven other spirits more evil than itself, and they go in and dwell there, and the last state of that person becomes worse than the first. So will it be with this evil generation also.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus' Mother and Brothers
\m
\v 46 While Jesus was still speaking to the crowds, behold, his mother and his brothers stood outside, asking to speak to him.
\v 47 \f + \fr 12:47 \fq Then … to speak to yoʋ.” \ft ¦ Then … to see yoʋ.” ANT ¦ — WH\f*Then someone said to him, “Behold, yoʋr mother and yoʋr brothers are standing outside, asking to speak to yoʋ.”
\v 48 But he replied to the one who spoke to him, \wj “Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?”\wj*
\v 49 And stretching out his hand toward his disciples, he said, \wj “Behold, my mother and my brothers!\wj*
\v 50 \wj For whoever does the will of my Father in heaven is my brother and sister and mother.”\wj*
\c 13
\s1 The Parable of the Sower
\m
\v 1 On that day Jesus went out of the house and sat by the sea,
\v 2 and large crowds gathered around him. So he got into \f + \fr 13:2 \fq the \ft ¦ a ANT CT PCK\f*the boat and sat down, while the entire crowd stood on the shore.
\v 3 Then he told them many things in parables, saying, \wj “Behold, a sower went out to sow.\wj*
\v 4 \wj As he sowed, some seeds fell along the path, and the birds came and devoured them.\wj*
\v 5 \wj Other seeds fell on rocky ground, where they did not have much soil, and they sprang up immediately because they had no depth of soil.\wj*
\v 6 \wj But when the sun rose, they were scorched. And because they had no root, they withered away.\wj*
\v 7 \wj Other seeds fell among the thorns, and the thorns grew up and choked them.\wj*
\v 8 \wj But other seeds fell on good soil and produced fruit, some a hundred, some sixty, and some thirty times more than what was sown.\wj*
\v 9 \wj He who has ears\wj* \f + \fr 13:9 \fq to hear \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj to hear, let him hear.”\wj*
\s1 The Purpose of Speaking in Parables
\m
\v 10 Then the disciples came and said to him, “Why do yoʋ speak to the people in parables?”
\v 11 Jesus answered \f + \fr 13:11 \fq them \ft ¦ — WH\f*them, \wj “To you it has been given to know the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven, but to them it has not been given.\wj*
\v 12 \wj For whoever has will be given more, and he will have an abundance. But whoever does not have, even what he has will be taken away from him.\wj*
\v 13 \wj That is why I speak to them in parables,\wj* \f + \fr 13:13 \fq because ‘they see but do not perceive, and they hear but do not listen or understand.’ In them is fulfilled the prophecy of Isaiah \ft ¦ so that ‘they may see but not perceive, and hear but not listen or understand, lest they should turn back.’ Then the prophecy of Isaiah will be fulfilled in them ANT\f*\wj because ‘they see but do not perceive, and they hear but do not listen or understand.’\wj*
\v 14 \wj In them is fulfilled the prophecy of Isaiah, which says,\wj*
\b
\q1 \wj ‘Yoʋ will hear but never understand,\wj*
\q1 \wj and you will see but never perceive.\wj*
\q1
\v 15 \wj For the heart of this people has become calloused;\wj*
\q1 \wj with their ears they have barely heard,\wj*
\q1 \wj and their eyes they have closed,\wj*
\q1 \wj lest they should see with their eyes,\wj*
\q1 \wj hear with their ears,\wj*
\q1 \wj and understand with their hearts and turn back,\wj*
\q1 \wj and I would heal them.’\wj*
\b
\m
\v 16 \wj But blessed are your eyes because they see, and your ears because they hear.\wj*
\v 17 \wj For truly I say to you, many prophets and righteous people desired to see the things you see but did not see them, and to hear the things you hear but did not hear them.\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Sower Explained
\m
\v 18 \wj “So hear what the parable of the sower means:\wj*
\v 19 \wj When anyone hears the word of the kingdom and does not understand it, the evil one comes and\wj* \f + \fr 13:19 \fq snatches \ft ¦ takes ANT\f*\wj snatches away what was sown in his heart. This is the seed sown along the path.\wj*
\v 20 \wj The seed sown on rocky ground is he who hears the word and immediately\wj* \f + \fr 13:20 \fq receives \ft ¦ accepts and receives ANT\f*\wj receives it with joy.\wj*
\v 21 \wj Yet he has no root in himself, but is only temporary. When tribulation or persecution arises on account of the word, he immediately falls away.\wj*
\v 22 \wj The seed sown among the thorns is he who hears the word, but the cares of\wj* \f + \fr 13:22 \fq this \ft ¦ the NA TH WH\f*\wj this world and the deceitfulness of riches choke the word, and it becomes unfruitful.\wj*
\v 23 \wj But the seed sown on the good soil is he who hears the word and understands it, who bears fruit and produces a hundred, sixty, or thirty times more than what was sown.”\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Wheat and the Tares
\m
\v 24 Jesus presented another parable to them, saying, \wj “The kingdom of heaven can be compared to a person who sowed good seed in his field,\wj*
\v 25 \wj but while people were sleeping, his enemy came and sowed tares among the wheat, and then went away.\wj*
\v 26 \wj When the plants sprouted and produced grain, the tares also appeared.\wj*
\v 27 \wj So the servants of the master of the house came and said to him, ‘Master, did yoʋ not sow good seed in yoʋr field? How then does it have tares?’\wj*
\v 28 \wj He said to them, ‘An enemy has done this.’ So the servants said to him, ‘Do yoʋ want us to go and gather them up?’\wj*
\v 29 \wj He said, ‘No, lest in gathering the tares you uproot the wheat with them.\wj*
\v 30 \wj Let them both grow together until the harvest, and at the time of the harvest I will say to the reapers, “Gather the tares first and bind them into bundles to burn them up, but gather the wheat into my barn.” ’ ”\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Mustard Seed
\m
\v 31 He presented another parable to them, saying, \wj “The kingdom of heaven is like a grain of mustard seed that a man took and sowed in his field.\wj*
\v 32 \wj Although it is the smallest of all seeds, it grows larger than\wj* \f + \fr 13:32 \fq all \ft ¦ — CT HF RP TR\f*\wj all the other garden plants and becomes a tree, and the birds of the sky come and nest in its branches.”\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Leaven
\m
\v 33 He spoke to them another parable: \wj “The kingdom of heaven is like leaven that a woman took and mixed into three measures of flour until it had all been leavened.”\wj*
\s1 The Use of Parables
\m
\v 34 All these things Jesus spoke to the crowds in parables; he \f + \fr 13:34 \fq did not speak \ft ¦ spoke nothing ANT CT\f*did not speak to them without using a parable.
\v 35 This was to fulfill what had been spoken through the prophet:
\b
\q1 “I will open my mouth in parables;
\q1 I will declare things hidden from the foundation \f + \fr 13:35 \fq of the world \ft 99.2% ¦ — SBL TH WH 0.6%\f*of the world.”
\s1 The Parable of the Wheat and the Tares Explained
\m
\v 36 Then Jesus left the crowds and went into \f + \fr 13:36 \fq the \ft ¦ his ANT\f*the house, and his disciples came to him and said, “Explain to us the parable of the tares of the field.”
\v 37 So he said \f + \fr 13:37 \fq to them \ft ¦ — CT\f*to them in response, \wj “He who sows the good seed is the Son of Man.\wj*
\v 38 \wj The field is the world, and the good seeds are the sons of the kingdom. The tares are the sons of the evil one,\wj*
\v 39 \wj and the enemy who sowed them is the devil. The harvest is the end of the age, and the reapers are angels.\wj*
\v 40 \wj Therefore, just as the tares are gathered and burned with fire, so it will be at the end of\wj* \f + \fr 13:40 \fq this \ft ¦ the CT\f*\wj this age.\wj*
\v 41 \wj The Son of Man will send his angels, and they will gather out of his kingdom all stumbling blocks and those who practice lawlessness.\wj*
\v 42 \wj They will throw them into the fiery furnace. In that place there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.\wj*
\v 43 \wj Then the righteous will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. He who has ears\wj* \f + \fr 13:43 \fq to hear \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj to hear, let him hear.\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Hidden Treasure
\m
\v 44 \wj “\wj*\f + \fr 13:44 \fq Again, the \ft ¦ The CT\f*\wj Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field, which a man found and hid again, and in his joy he went and sold all that he had and bought the field.\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Pearl of Great Price
\m
\v 45 \wj “Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking fine pearls.\wj*
\v 46 \wj When he found one pearl of great price, he went and sold all that he had and bought it.\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Dragnet
\m
\v 47 \wj “Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a dragnet that was cast into the sea, catching all kinds of fish.\wj*
\v 48 \wj When it was full, the fishermen drew it to shore. Then they sat down and put the good fish into containers, but the bad ones they threw away.\wj*
\v 49 \wj So it will be at the end of the age. The angels will come and separate the evil from the righteous\wj*
\v 50 \wj and throw them into the fiery furnace. In that place there will be weeping and gnashing of\wj* \f + \fr 13:50 \fq teeth.” Jesus said to them, \ft ¦ teeth. CT\f*\wj teeth.”\wj*
\s1 New and Old Treasures
\m
\v 51 Jesus said to them, \wj “Do you understand all these things?”\wj* They said to him, “\f + \fr 13:51 \fq Yes, Lord. \ft ¦ Yes. CT\f*Yes, Lord.”
\v 52 Then he said to them, \wj “Every scribe that has been trained as a disciple for the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a house who brings out of his treasure things new and old.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Rejected at Nazareth
\m
\v 53 When Jesus had finished these parables, he departed from there.
\v 54 Then he went to his hometown and taught the people in their synagogue. They were astonished and said, “Where did this man get this wisdom and these miraculous powers?
\v 55 Is this not the carpenter's son? Is not his mother's name Mary, and are not his brothers James, \f + \fr 13:55 \fq Joses \ft ¦ Joseph CT\f*Joses, Simon, and Judas?
\v 56 Are not all his sisters with us? Where then did this man get all these things?”
\v 57 And they took offense at him. Then Jesus said to them, \wj “A prophet is not without honor except in his hometown and in his own household.”\wj*
\v 58 And he did not do many miracles there because of their unbelief.
\c 14
\s1 The Death of John the Baptist
\m
\v 1 At that time Herod the tetrarch heard the report about Jesus
\v 2 and said to his servants, “This man is John the Baptist; he has been raised from the dead, and that is why these mighty powers are at work in him.”
\v 3 For Herod had arrested John, bound him, and put him in prison on account of Herodias, his brother Philip's wife.
\v 4 For John had been saying to him, “It is not lawful for yoʋ to have her.”
\v 5 Although Herod wanted to kill John, he was afraid of the crowd, because they regarded John as a prophet.
\v 6 But when Herod's birthday \f + \fr 14:6 \fq was celebrated \ft ¦ came CT\f*was celebrated, the daughter of Herodias danced before the guests and pleased Herod.
\v 7 So he promised with an oath to give her whatever she might ask.
\v 8 Prompted by her mother, she said, “Give me here on a platter the head of John the Baptist.”
\v 9 The king was grieved, but because of his oaths and his guests, he commanded it to be given
\v 10 and sent orders to have John beheaded in prison.
\v 11 John's head was then brought on a platter and given to the girl, and she brought it to her mother.
\v 12 Later, John's disciples came and took his body and buried it. Then they went and brought word to Jesus.
\s1 The Feeding of the Five Thousand
\m
\v 13 When Jesus heard this, he withdrew from there in a boat to a desolate place by himself. But the crowds heard about it and followed him on foot from the towns.
\v 14 And when Jesus went ashore and saw a large crowd, he was moved with compassion for them and healed their sick.
\v 15 As evening approached, \f + \fr 14:15 \fq his \ft ¦ the CT\f*his disciples came to him and said, “This place is desolate, and the hour is now late. Send the crowds away so that they can go into the villages and buy themselves some food.”
\v 16 But Jesus said to them, \wj “They have no need to go away; you give them something to eat.”\wj*
\v 17 They said to him, “We have here only five loaves and two fish.”
\v 18 He said, \wj “Bring them here to me.”\wj*
\v 19 Then he commanded the crowds to sit down on the grass, and taking the five loaves and the two fish, he looked up to heaven and said the blessing. Then he broke the loaves and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the crowds.
\v 20 They all ate and were filled, and the disciples picked up what was left over of the broken pieces, twelve baskets full.
\v 21 Now those who had eaten were about five thousand men, besides women and children.
\s1 Jesus Walks on Water
\m
\v 22 Immediately Jesus compelled \f + \fr 14:22 \fq his \ft ¦ the CT HF RP\f*the disciples to get into \f + \fr 14:22 \fq the \ft ¦ a SBL WH\f*the boat and go ahead of him to the other side of the sea, while he sent the crowds away.
\v 23 After sending the crowds away, he went up on the mountain by himself to pray. When evening came, he was there alone.
\v 24 Meanwhile, the boat was already \f + \fr 14:24 \fq in the middle of the sea \ft ¦ some distance from the land NA SBL WH\f*in the middle of the sea, being tossed by the waves, for the wind was against them.
\v 25 And in the fourth watch of the night Jesus \f + \fr 14:25 \fq went \ft ¦ came CT\f*went to them, walking on the sea.
\v 26 When the disciples saw him walking on the sea, they were terrified and said, “It is a ghost!” And they cried out in fear.
\v 27 But Jesus immediately said to them, \wj “Take courage! It is I; do not be afraid.”\wj*
\p
\v 28 In response Peter said \f + \fr 14:28 \fq to him \ft ¦ — PCK\f*to him, “Lord, if it is yoʋ, command me to come to yoʋ on the water.”
\v 29 So he said, \wj “Come.”\wj* Then Peter got down from the boat and walked on the water \f + \fr 14:29 \fq to go \ft ¦ and came CT\f*to go to Jesus.
\v 30 But when he saw the \f + \fr 14:30 \fq strong \ft ¦ — WH\f*strong wind, he became frightened, and beginning to sink, he cried out, “Lord, save me!”
\v 31 Immediately Jesus reached out his hand, took hold of him, and said to him, \wj “O yoʋ of little faith, why did yoʋ doubt?”\wj*
\v 32 And when they had gotten into the boat, the wind ceased.
\v 33 Then those who were in the boat \f + \fr 14:33 \fq came and \ft ¦ — CT\f*came and worshiped him, saying, “Truly yoʋ are the Son of God.”
\s1 Jesus Heals the Sick in Gennesaret
\m
\v 34 When they had crossed the sea, they came to \f + \fr 14:34 \fq the land of \ft ¦ land at CT\f*the land of Gennesaret.
\v 35 And when the men of that place recognized Jesus, they sent word into the entire surrounding region, and people brought to him all who were sick.
\v 36 They begged him to let the sick touch even the fringe of his garment, and all who touched it were completely healed.
\c 15
\s1 The Traditions of the Elders
\m
\v 1 Then \f + \fr 15:1 \fq the scribes and Pharisees \ft ¦ some Pharisees and scribes CT\f*the scribes and Pharisees from Jerusalem came to Jesus and said,
\v 2 “Why do yoʋr disciples break the tradition of the elders? For they do not wash their hands when they eat bread.”
\v 3 He answered them, \wj “And why do you break the commandment of God for the sake of your tradition?\wj*
\v 4 \wj For God\wj* \f + \fr 15:4 \fq commanded \ft ¦ said CT\f*\wj commanded, ‘Honor yoʋr father and yoʋr mother,’ and, ‘Whoever speaks evil of his father or mother must surely die.’\wj*
\v 5 \wj But you say, ‘If anyone says to his father or mother, “Whatever benefit yoʋ might have received from me is now a gift devoted to God,”\wj* \f + \fr 15:5 \fq then \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj then he certainly need not honor his father\wj* \f + \fr 15:5 \fq or his mother \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj or his mother.’\wj*
\v 6 \wj You have nullified the\wj* \f + \fr 15:6 \fq commandment \ft 98.2% ¦ word CT 0.5%\f*\wj commandment of God for the sake of your tradition.\wj*
\v 7 \wj Hypocrites! Isaiah prophesied rightly about you when he said,\wj*
\b
\q1
\v 8 \wj ‘This people\wj* \f + \fr 15:8 \fq draws near to me with their mouth and \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj draws near to me with their mouth\wj*
\q1 \wj and honors me with their lips,\wj*
\q1 \wj but their heart is far from me.\wj*
\q1
\v 9 \wj They worship me in vain,\wj*
\q1 \wj teaching as doctrines the commandments of men.’ ”\wj*
\b
\p
\v 10 Then Jesus called the crowd over and said to them, \wj “Listen and understand:\wj*
\v 11 \wj It is not what goes into the mouth that defiles a person; it is what comes out of the mouth that defiles a person.”\wj*
\v 12 Then \f + \fr 15:12 \fq his \ft ¦ the NA SBL WH\f*his disciples came and said to him, “Do yoʋ know that the Pharisees were offended when they heard what yoʋ said?”
\v 13 Jesus answered, \wj “Every plant that my heavenly Father has not planted will be uprooted.\wj*
\v 14 \wj Leave them be; they are blind guides\wj* \f + \fr 15:14 \fq of the blind \ft ¦ — WH\f*\wj of the blind. And if one blind person guides another, both will fall into a pit.”\wj*
\p
\v 15 Then Peter said to him in response, “Explain \f + \fr 15:15 \fq this \ft ¦ the TH WH\f*this parable to us.”
\v 16 Jesus said, \wj “Are you also still without understanding?\wj*
\v 17 \wj Do you not\wj* \f + \fr 15:17 \fq yet \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj yet understand that everything that goes into the mouth passes into the stomach and is expelled into the latrine?\wj*
\v 18 \wj But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart, and these defile a person.\wj*
\v 19 \wj For from the heart come evil thoughts, murder, adultery, fornication, theft, false testimony, and slander.\wj*
\v 20 \wj These are what defile a person, but to eat with unwashed hands does not defile a person.”\wj*
\s1 A Canaanite Woman's Faith
\m
\v 21 Then Jesus went away from there and withdrew to the district of Tyre and Sidon.
\v 22 And behold, a Canaanite woman from that region came and cried out \f + \fr 15:22 \fq to him \ft ¦ — CT\f*to him, “Have mercy on me, Lord, Son of David! My daughter is severely demon-possessed.”
\v 23 But he did not respond to her at all. So his disciples came to him and urged him, “Send her away, for she is crying out after us.”
\v 24 In response Jesus said, \wj “I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.”\wj*
\v 25 But she came and bowed down before him, saying, “Lord, help me.”
\v 26 He replied, \wj “It is not good to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs.”\wj*
\v 27 She said, “Yes, Lord, yet even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from the table of their masters.”
\v 28 Then Jesus answered her, \wj “O woman, great is yoʋr faith; it will be done for yoʋ as yoʋ wish.”\wj* And her daughter was healed from that hour.
\s1 Jesus Heals Many
\m
\v 29 Departing from there, Jesus went along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up on the mountain and sat down.
\v 30 Large crowds came to him, having with them the \f + \fr 15:30 \fq lame, blind, mute, crippled \ft ¦ lame, blind, crippled, mute NA ¦ mute, blind, lame, crippled SBL ¦ lame, mute, blind, crippled TH \ft ¦ lame, crippled, blind, mute WH\f*lame, blind, mute, crippled, and many others. They put them at the feet of Jesus, and he healed them.
\v 31 So the \f + \fr 15:31 \fq crowds were \ft ¦ crowd was CT\f*crowds were amazed when they \f + \fr 15:31 \fq saw \ft ¦ saw the deaf hearing, ANT\f*saw the mute speaking, \f + \fr 15:31 \fq the crippled made well, \ft ¦ — WH\f*the crippled made well, the lame walking, and the blind seeing. And they glorified the God of Israel. \fig Large crowds came to him, having with them the lame, blind, mute, crippled, and many others. They put them at the feet of Jesus, and he healed them.|src="01 Matthew 15_30.png" size="span" copy="Public Domain (Gustave Doré)" ref="15:30" \fig*
\s1 The Feeding of the Four Thousand
\m
\v 32 Then Jesus called his disciples over and said, \wj “I have compassion on the crowd because they have stayed with me now for three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away without having eaten, lest they faint on the way.”\wj*
\v 33 \f + \fr 15:33 \fq His \ft ¦ The CT\f*His disciples said to him, “Where can we get so many loaves in such a desolate place to fill such a large crowd?”
\v 34 Jesus said to them, \wj “How many loaves do you have?”\wj* They said, “Seven, and a few small fish.”
\v 35 So he commanded the crowds to sit down on the ground,
\v 36 and taking the seven loaves and the fish, he gave thanks, broke them, and gave them to \f + \fr 15:36 \fq his \ft ¦ the CT\f*his disciples, and the disciples gave them to the \f + \fr 15:36 \fq crowd \ft ¦ crowds ANT CT\f*crowd.
\v 37 They all ate and were filled, and the disciples picked up what was left over of the broken pieces, seven baskets full.
\v 38 Besides women and children, there were four thousand men who had eaten.
\v 39 After sending the crowds away, Jesus got into the boat and went to the region of \f + \fr 15:39 \fq Magdala \ft ¦ Magadan CT\f*Magdala.
\c 16
\s1 The Pharisees and Sadducees Ask For a Sign
\m
\v 1 Now the Pharisees and Sadducees came and tested Jesus, asking him to show them a sign from heaven.
\v 2 But he answered them, \wj “When evening comes you say, ‘It will be fair weather, for the sky is red.’\wj*
\v 3 \wj And in the morning you say, ‘There will be a storm today, for the sky is red and threatening.’\wj* \f + \fr 16:3 \fq Hypocrites! \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj Hypocrites! You know how to discern the appearance of the sky, but you cannot\wj* \f + \fr 16:3 \fq discern \ft ¦ recognize ANT\f*\wj discern the signs of the times?\wj*
\v 4 \wj An evil and adulterous generation asks for a sign, but no sign will be given to it except the sign of\wj* \f + \fr 16:4 \fq the prophet \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj the prophet Jonah.”\wj* Then he left them and went away.
\s1 The Leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees
\m
\v 5 When \f + \fr 16:5 \fq his \ft ¦ the CT\f*his disciples reached the other side of the sea, they realized that they had forgotten to bring bread.
\v 6 Then Jesus said to them, \wj “Watch out and beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.”\wj*
\v 7 So they discussed this among themselves, saying, “He must have said this because we brought no bread.”
\v 8 Aware of this, Jesus said \f + \fr 16:8 \fq to them \ft ¦ — CT\f*to them, \wj “Why are you discussing among yourselves, O you of little faith, the fact that you\wj* \f + \fr 16:8 \fq brought \ft 97.4% ¦ have NA TH WH 1.7%\f*\wj brought no bread?\wj*
\v 9 \wj Do you not yet understand? Do you not remember the five loaves for the five thousand, and how many baskets you collected?\wj*
\v 10 \wj Or the seven loaves for the four thousand, and how many baskets you collected?\wj*
\v 11 \wj How could you not understand that I was not\wj* \f + \fr 16:11 \fq talking about bread when I told you to beware … Sadducees? \ft ¦ speaking to you about bread? But beware … Sadducees. CT\f*\wj talking about bread when I told you to beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees?”\wj*
\v 12 Then they understood that he had not told them to beware of the leaven of bread, but of the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
\s1 Peter Declares That Jesus Is the Christ
\m
\v 13 Now when Jesus came into the district of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, \wj “Who do people say that\wj* \f + \fr 16:13 \fq I, the Son of Man, am \ft ¦ the Son of Man is CT\f*\wj I, the Son of Man, am?”\wj*
\v 14 They said, “Some say John the Baptist, others say Elijah, and others say that yoʋ are Jeremiah or one of the prophets.”
\v 15 He said to them, \wj “But who do you say that I am?”\wj*
\v 16 Simon Peter answered, “Yoʋ are the Christ, the Son of the living God.”
\v 17 And Jesus answered him, \wj “Blessed are yoʋ, Simon Bar-Jonah, for flesh and blood has not revealed this to yoʋ, but my Father in heaven.\wj*
\v 18 \wj And I tell yoʋ that yoʋ are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church, and the gates of Hades shall not prevail against it.\wj*
\v 19 \wj I will give yoʋ the keys of the kingdom of heaven, and whatever yoʋ bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever yoʋ loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.”\wj*
\v 20 Then he \f + \fr 16:20 \fq commanded \ft ¦ warned WH\f*commanded \f + \fr 16:20 \fq his \ft ¦ the CT\f*his disciples to tell no one that he was \f + \fr 16:20 \fq Jesus \ft ¦ — CT PCK\f*Jesus the Christ.
\s1 Jesus Predicts His Death and Resurrection
\m
\v 21 From that time \f + \fr 16:21 \fq Jesus \ft ¦ Jesus Christ WH\f*Jesus began to show his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things at the hands of the elders, chief priests, and scribes, and that he must be killed and on the third day be raised up.
\v 22 So Peter took him aside and began to rebuke him, saying, “God forbid, Lord! This shall certainly not happen to yoʋ.”
\v 23 But he turned around and said to Peter, \wj “Get behind me, Satan! Yoʋ are a stumbling block to me, for yoʋ are not thinking about the things of God, but the things of men.”\wj*
\p
\v 24 Then Jesus said to his disciples, \wj “If anyone wants to be one of my disciples, he must deny himself, take up his cross, and follow me.\wj*
\v 25 \wj For whoever wants to save his life will lose it, but whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.\wj*
\v 26 \wj For what\wj* \f + \fr 16:26 \fq does \ft ¦ will CT\f*\wj does it benefit a person if he gains the whole world but forfeits his soul? Or what can a person give in exchange for his soul?\wj*
\v 27 \wj For the Son of Man will come with his angels in the glory of his Father, and then he will repay each person according to what he has done.\wj*
\v 28 \wj Truly I say to you, there are some standing here who will certainly not taste death until they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.”\wj*
\c 17
\s1 The Transfiguration
\m
\v 1 After six days Jesus took Peter, James, and John the brother of James, and led them up on a high mountain by themselves.
\v 2 There he was transfigured before them; his face shone like the sun, and his garments became as white as light.
\v 3 And behold, Moses and Elijah appeared to them, talking with him.
\v 4 So Peter said to Jesus, “Lord, it is good for us to be here. If yoʋ want, \f + \fr 17:4 \fq we \ft ¦ I CT\f*we will make three shelters here, one for yoʋ, one for Moses, and one for Elijah.”
\v 5 While he was still speaking, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice from the cloud said, “This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; listen to him!”
\v 6 When the disciples heard this, they fell on their faces and were utterly afraid.
\v 7 But Jesus came over and touched them, saying, \wj “Rise; do not be afraid.”\wj*
\v 8 And lifting up their eyes, they saw no one but Jesus.
\p
\v 9 As they were coming down from the mountain, Jesus commanded them, \wj “Do not tell anyone about the vision you saw until the Son of Man has\wj* \f + \fr 17:9 \fq risen \ft ¦ been raised NA SBL WH\f*\wj risen from the dead.”\wj*
\v 10 Then \f + \fr 17:10 \fq his \ft ¦ the NA SBL WH\f*his disciples asked him, “Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?”
\v 11 Jesus answered \f + \fr 17:11 \fq them \ft ¦ — CT\f*them, \wj “Elijah does indeed come\wj* \f + \fr 17:11 \fq first \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj first, and he will restore all things.\wj*
\v 12 \wj But I tell you that Elijah has already come, and they did not recognize him, but did to him whatever they wanted. Even so the Son of Man is about to suffer at their hands.”\wj*
\v 13 Then the disciples understood that he was speaking to them about John the Baptist.
\s1 Jesus Heals a Demon-Possessed Boy
\m
\v 14 When they came to the crowd, a man approached Jesus, kneeling before him
\v 15 and saying, “Lord, have mercy on my son, for he is an epileptic and \f + \fr 17:15 \fq suffers terribly \ft 99.4% ¦ is sick TH WH 0.6%\f*suffers terribly. For often he falls into the fire, and often into the water.
\v 16 I brought him to yoʋr disciples, but they could not heal him.”
\v 17 Jesus answered, \wj “O faithless and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? How long shall I bear with you? Bring him here to me.”\wj*
\v 18 Then Jesus rebuked the demon, and it came out of the boy, and he was healed from that hour.
\v 19 Afterward the disciples came to Jesus privately and said, “Why could we not cast it out?”
\v 20 Jesus said to them, \wj “Because of your\wj* \f + \fr 17:20 \fq unbelief \ft 97.6% ¦ little faith CT 1.5%\f*\wj unbelief. For truly I say to you, if you have faith like a grain of mustard seed, you will say to this mountain, ‘Move from here to there,’ and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you.\wj*
\v 21 \f + \fr 17:21 \fq But this kind does not go out except by prayer and fasting. \ft 97.6% ¦ — CT 0.6%\f*\wj But this kind does not go out except by prayer and fasting.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Predicts His Death and Resurrection a Second Time
\m
\v 22 While they were \f + \fr 17:22 \fq staying \ft ¦ gathering together CT\f*staying in Galilee, Jesus said to them, \wj “The Son of Man is about to be delivered up into the hands of men.\wj*
\v 23 \wj They will kill him, and on the third day he will be raised up.”\wj* And the disciples were greatly distressed.
\s1 Paying the Temple Tax
\m
\v 24 When they came to Capernaum, the collectors of the two-drachma tax came to Peter and said, “Your teacher pays the two-drachma tax, doesn't he?”
\v 25 He said, “Yes.” And when \f + \fr 17:25 \fq Peter \ft ¦ they PCK\f*Peter came into the house, Jesus spoke to him first, saying, \wj “What do yoʋ think, Simon? From whom do the kings of the earth receive toll or tax? From their own sons or from strangers?”\wj*
\v 26 \f + \fr 17:26 \fq Peter said to him, “From strangers.” And \ft ¦ When he said, “From strangers,” CT\f*Peter said to him, “From strangers.” And Jesus said to him, \wj “Then the sons are free.\wj*
\v 27 \wj But so that we may not offend them, go to the sea, cast a hook, and take the first fish yoʋ catch. When yoʋ open its mouth, yoʋ will find a stater coin. Take it and give it to them for yoʋ and me.”\wj*
\c 18
\s1 True Greatness
\m
\v 1 In that hour the disciples came to Jesus and said, “Who is greater in the kingdom of heaven?”
\v 2 So Jesus called over a little child, placed him among them,
\v 3 and said, \wj “Truly I say to you, unless you change and become like little children, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven.\wj*
\v 4 \wj Therefore, whoever humbles himself like this little child is greater in the kingdom of heaven.\wj*
\v 5 \wj And whoever receives one little child such as this in my name receives me.\wj*
\v 6 \wj But if anyone causes one of these little ones who believe in me to stumble, it would be better for him to have a heavy millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea.\wj*
\s1 Temptations to Sin
\m
\v 7 \wj “Woe to the world because of stumbling blocks! It is necessary that stumbling blocks come, yet woe to\wj* \f + \fr 18:7 \fq that \ft ¦ the CT\f*\wj that man through whom the stumbling block comes!\wj*
\v 8 \wj If yoʋr hand or yoʋr foot causes yoʋ to stumble, cut\wj* \f + \fr 18:8 \fq them off and throw them \ft ¦ it off and throw it CT\f*\wj them off and throw them away from yoʋ. It is better for yoʋ to enter life\wj* \f + \fr 18:8 \fq lame or crippled \ft ¦ crippled or lame NA SBL WH\f*\wj lame or crippled than to have two hands or two feet and be thrown into the eternal fire.\wj*
\v 9 \wj And if yoʋr eye causes yoʋ to stumble, pluck it out and throw it away from yoʋ. It is better for yoʋ to enter life with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into the fires of hell.\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Lost Sheep
\m
\v 10 \wj “Be careful not to despise one of these little ones, for I tell you that their angels in heaven continually see the face of my Father in heaven.\wj*
\v 11 \f + \fr 18:11 \fq For the Son of Man came to save the lost. \ft 82.6% ¦ For the Son of Man came to seek and save the lost. \ft MSS 15.2% \ft ¦ — CT 1.5%\f*\wj For the Son of Man came to save the lost.\wj*
\v 12 \wj What do you think? If a man has a hundred sheep, and one of them goes astray,\wj* \f + \fr 18:12 \fq does \ft ¦ will CT\f*\wj does he not leave the other ninety-nine\wj* \f + \fr 18:12 \fq and go seek on the mountains \ft ¦ on the mountains and go seek CT\f*\wj and go seek on the mountains the one that has gone astray?\wj*
\v 13 \wj And if he happens to find it, truly I say to you, he rejoices more over it than over the ninety-nine that have not gone astray.\wj*
\v 14 \wj In the same way, it is not the will of\wj* \f + \fr 18:14 \fq your \ft ¦ my WH\f*\wj your Father in heaven that one of these little ones should perish.\wj*
\s1 Restoring a Brother Who Sins Against Yoʋ
\m
\v 15 \wj “If yoʋr brother sins\wj* \f + \fr 18:15 \fq against yoʋ \ft ¦ — WH\f*\wj against yoʋ, go show him his fault between yoʋ and him alone. If he listens to yoʋ, yoʋ have gained yoʋr brother.\wj*
\v 16 \wj But if he does not listen, take one or two others with yoʋ, so that every matter may be established by the mouth of two or three witnesses.\wj*
\v 17 \wj If he refuses to listen to them, tell it to the church. And if he refuses to listen even to the church, treat him as you would a pagan or a tax collector.\wj*
\v 18 \wj Truly I say to you, whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.\wj*
\v 19 \f + \fr 18:19 \fq Again, truly \ft ¦ Again TR\f*\wj Again, truly I say to you, if two of you agree on earth about anything you ask, it will be done for you by my Father in heaven.\wj*
\v 20 \wj For where two or three are gathered together in my name, I am there among them.”\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Unforgiving Servant
\m
\v 21 Then Peter came \f + \fr 18:21 \fq to Jesus and said \ft ¦ and said to Jesus NA WH\f*to Jesus and said, “Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he keeps sinning against me? Up to seven times?”
\v 22 Jesus said to him, \wj “Not, I tell yoʋ, up to seven times, but up to seventy times seven.\wj*
\p
\v 23 \wj “For this reason the kingdom of heaven may be compared to a king who wished to settle accounts with his servants.\wj*
\v 24 \wj As he began to settle accounts, one servant who had a debt of ten thousand talents was brought to him.\wj*
\v 25 \wj But since he did not have anything with which to repay the debt, his master commanded him to be sold along with his wife, his children, and all that he had, so that the debt could be repaid.\wj*
\v 26 \wj So the servant fell down and bowed before him, saying, ‘\wj*\f + \fr 18:26 \fq Master, be \ft ¦ Be NA SBL WH\f*\wj Master, be patient with me, and I will repay yoʋ all that I owe.’\wj*
\v 27 \wj Moved with compassion, the master of that servant released him and forgave him the debt.\wj*
\v 28 \wj But that servant went out and found one of his fellow servants who owed him a hundred denarii. He grabbed him and began to choke him, saying, ‘Repay\wj* \f + \fr 18:28 \fq me \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj me what yoʋ owe.’\wj*
\v 29 \wj So his fellow servant fell down\wj* \f + \fr 18:29 \fq at his feet \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj at his feet and begged him, ‘Be patient with me, and I will repay\wj* \f + \fr 18:29 \fq yoʋ \ft ¦ yoʋ everything TR\f*\wj yoʋ.’\wj*
\v 30 \wj But he was not willing to do so. Instead, he went and threw him into prison until he should repay what was owed.\wj*
\v 31 \wj When his fellow servants saw what had taken place, they were greatly distressed and went and reported to their master all that had happened.\wj*
\v 32 \wj Then the master summoned the servant and said to him, ‘Yoʋ evil servant! I forgave yoʋ all that debt because yoʋ begged me to do so.\wj*
\v 33 \wj Should yoʋ not also have had mercy on yoʋr fellow servant, even as I had mercy on yoʋ?’\wj*
\v 34 \wj And in anger his master handed him over to the jailers to be tortured until he should repay all that was owed\wj* \f + \fr 18:34 \fq him \ft ¦ — NA SBL WH\f*\wj him.\wj*
\v 35 \wj My heavenly Father will also do the same to you if each one of you, from your hearts, does not forgive his brother\wj* \f + \fr 18:35 \fq for his trespasses \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj for his trespasses.”\wj*
\c 19
\s1 Questions About Divorce
\m
\v 1 When Jesus had finished saying these things, he departed from Galilee and came into the region of Judea beyond the Jordan.
\v 2 Large crowds followed him, and he healed them there.
\p
\v 3 Then \f + \fr 19:3 \fq the \ft ¦ some CT\f*the Pharisees came up to Jesus and tested him by asking \f + \fr 19:3 \fq him \ft ¦ — CT\f*him, “Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any reason?”
\v 4 He answered \f + \fr 19:4 \fq them \ft ¦ — CT\f*them, \wj “Have you not read that he who\wj* \f + \fr 19:4 \fq made \ft ¦ created CT\f*\wj made them from the beginning ‘made them male and female,’\wj*
\v 5 \wj and said, ‘For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and the two will become one flesh’?\wj*
\v 6 \wj So then, they are no longer two, but one flesh. Therefore what God has joined together, let no one separate.”\wj*
\v 7 They said to him, “Why then did Moses command us to give her a certificate of divorce and send her away?”
\v 8 He said to them, \wj “Because of your hardness of heart Moses allowed you to divorce your wives, but from the beginning it was not so.\wj*
\v 9 \wj I tell you that whoever divorces his wife, except for fornication, and marries another commits adultery.\wj* \f + \fr 19:9 \fq And whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. \ft ¦ — NA WH\f*\wj And whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery.”\wj*
\p
\v 10 \f + \fr 19:10 \fq His \ft ¦ The SBL WH\f*His disciples said to him, “If such is the case of a man with his wife, it is better not to marry.”
\v 11 But Jesus said to them, \wj “Not all men can accept this statement, but only those to whom it is given.\wj*
\v 12 \wj For there are eunuchs who were born that way from their mother's womb, and there are eunuchs who have been made eunuchs by men, and there are eunuchs who have made themselves eunuchs for the sake of the kingdom of heaven. Whoever is able to accept this should accept it.”\wj*
\s1 Let the Little Children Come to Me
\m
\v 13 Then little children were brought to Jesus so that he might lay his hands on them and pray, but the disciples rebuked those who brought them.
\v 14 Yet Jesus said, \wj “Let the little children come to me and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these.”\wj*
\v 15 And after laying his hands on them, he departed from there.
\s1 The Rich Young Man
\m
\v 16 And behold, a man came \f + \fr 19:16 \fq and said to him \ft ¦ to him and said CT\f*and said to him, “\f + \fr 19:16 \fq Good \ft ¦ — CT\f*Good Teacher, what good thing must I do to have eternal life?”
\v 17 Jesus said to him, \wj “Why\wj* \f + \fr 19:17 \fq do yoʋ call me good? No one is good except God alone \ft 97.7% ¦ do yoʋ ask me about what is good? There is only one who is good CT 0.2%\f*\wj do yoʋ call me good? No one is good except God alone. But if yoʋ wish to enter life, keep the commandments.”\wj*
\v 18 The man said to him, “Which ones?” Jesus said, \wj “ ‘Yoʋ shall not murder, yoʋ shall not commit adultery, yoʋ shall not steal, yoʋ shall not give false testimony,\wj*
\v 19 \wj honor yoʋr father and yoʋr mother,’ and, ‘Yoʋ shall love yoʋr neighbor as yoʋrself.’ ”\wj*
\v 20 The young man said to him, “I have kept all these commandments \f + \fr 19:20 \fq from the time I was a young boy \ft ¦ — CT\f*from the time I was a young boy; what do I still lack?”
\v 21 Jesus said to him, \wj “If yoʋ wish to be perfect, go sell what yoʋ have and give to the poor, and yoʋ will have treasure in heaven; then come follow me.”\wj*
\v 22 But when the young man heard this, he went away sorrowful, for he had many possessions.
\p
\v 23 Then Jesus said to his disciples, \wj “Truly I say to you, only with difficulty will a rich person enter the kingdom of heaven.\wj*
\v 24 \wj Again I say to you, it is easier for a camel to\wj* \wj go through the eye of a needle than for a rich person to enter the kingdom of God.”\wj*
\v 25 When \f + \fr 19:25 \fq his \ft ¦ the CT\f*his disciples heard this, they were greatly astonished and said, “Who then can be saved?”
\v 26 Jesus looked at them and said to them, \wj “With men it is impossible, but with God all things are possible.”\wj*
\v 27 Then Peter said to him in response, “Behold, we have left everything and followed yoʋ; what then will there be for us?”
\v 28 Jesus said to them, \wj “Truly I say to you, when all things are made new and the Son of Man sits on his throne of glory, you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.\wj*
\v 29 \wj And everyone who has left houses, brothers, sisters, father, mother,\wj* \f + \fr 19:29 \fq wife, \ft ¦ — NA WH\f*\wj wife, children, or fields for my name's sake, will receive\wj* \f + \fr 19:29 \fq a hundred \ft ¦ many WH\f*\wj a hundred times more and will inherit eternal life.\wj*
\v 30 \wj But many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.\wj*
\c 20
\s1 The Parable of the Workers in the Vineyard
\m
\v 1 \wj “For the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a house who went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.\wj*
\v 2 \wj After agreeing with the workers that they would each receive a denarius for the day, he sent them into his vineyard.\wj*
\v 3 \wj About the third hour he went out and saw others standing idle in the marketplace.\wj*
\v 4 \wj He said to them, ‘You go into my vineyard as well, and I will give you whatever is right.’\wj*
\v 5 \wj So they went. He went out again about the sixth hour and the ninth hour and did likewise.\wj*
\v 6 \wj About the eleventh hour he went out and found others standing\wj* \f + \fr 20:6 \fq idle \ft ¦ around CT\f*\wj idle and said to them, ‘Why have you been standing here idle all day?’\wj*
\v 7 \wj They said to him, ‘Because no one has hired us.’ He said to them, ‘You go into my vineyard as\wj* \f + \fr 20:7 \fq well, and you will receive whatever is right. \ft ¦ well. CT\f*\wj well, and you will receive whatever is right.’\wj*
\p
\v 8 \wj “When evening came, the owner of the vineyard said to his foreman, ‘Call the workers and pay\wj* \f + \fr 20:8 \fq them \ft ¦ — WH\f*\wj them their wages, beginning with the last and going on to the first.’\wj*
\v 9 \wj When those who had been hired about the eleventh hour came, they each received a denarius.\wj*
\v 10 \wj So when those who had been hired first came, they thought they would receive more, but they also received a denarius each.\wj*
\v 11 \wj When they received it, they grumbled against the master of the house,\wj*
\v 12 \wj saying, ‘These last men worked for one hour, and yoʋ have made them equal to us, who have borne the burden of the day and the scorching heat.’\wj*
\v 13 \wj But he replied to one of them, ‘Friend, I am doing yoʋ no wrong. Did yoʋ not agree with me to work for a denarius?\wj*
\v 14 \wj Take what is yoʋrs and go. I wish to give to this last man just as I gave to yoʋ.\wj*
\v 15 \f + \fr 20:15 \fq Or is \ft ¦ Is SBL WH\f*\wj Or is it not lawful for me to do what I wish with what is mine?\wj* \f + \fr 20:15 \fq Are \ft ¦ Or are CT ELZ\f*\wj Are yoʋ envious because I am good?’\wj*
\v 16 \wj So the last will be first, and the first will be last.\wj* \f + \fr 20:16 \fq For many are called, but few are chosen. \ft 97.5% ¦ — CT 1.4%\f*\wj For many are called, but few are chosen.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Predicts His Death and Resurrection a Third Time
\m
\v 17 As Jesus was \f + \fr 20:17 \fq going \ft ¦ about to go WH\f*going up to Jerusalem, he took the twelve disciples aside \f + \fr 20:17 \fq privately on the way and \ft ¦ privately, and on the way he CT\f*privately on the way and said to them,
\v 18 \wj “Behold, we are going up to Jerusalem, and the Son of Man will be delivered up to the chief priests and scribes. They will condemn him to death\wj*
\v 19 \wj and hand him over to the Gentiles to be mocked, flogged, and crucified. But on the third day he will\wj* \f + \fr 20:19 \fq rise again \ft ¦ be raised up CT\f*\wj rise again.”\wj*
\s1 The Request of the Mother of James and John
\m
\v 20 Then the mother of the sons of Zebedee came with her sons to Jesus, and bowing down she asked him for a favor.
\v 21 So he said to her, \wj “What do yoʋ want?”\wj* She said to him, “Declare that one of these two sons of mine may sit on yoʋr right and the other on yoʋr left in yoʋr kingdom.”
\v 22 Jesus answered, \wj “You do not know what you are asking. Are you able to drink the cup that I am about to\wj* \f + \fr 20:22 \fq drink, or to be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with? \ft ¦ drink, and to be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with? TR ¦ drink? CT\f*\wj drink, or to be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with?”\wj* The two brothers said to him, “We are able.”
\v 23 So he said to them, \wj “You will indeed drink my\wj* \f + \fr 20:23 \fq cup and be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with, \ft ¦ cup, CT\f*\wj cup and be baptized with the baptism that I am baptized with, but to sit on my right and on my left is not mine to give, but is for those for whom it has been prepared by my Father.”\wj*
\v 24 When the ten heard this, they were indignant with the two brothers.
\v 25 So Jesus called them over and said, \wj “You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great men exercise authority over them.\wj*
\v 26 \wj But it\wj* \f + \fr 20:26 \fq shall not be \ft ¦ is not WH\f*\wj shall not be so among you. Instead, whoever wants to be great among you must be your servant,\wj*
\v 27 \wj and whoever wants to be first among you must be your slave,\wj*
\v 28 \wj just as the Son of Man came not to be served but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Heals Two Blind Men
\m
\v 29 As they were leaving Jericho, a large crowd followed him.
\v 30 And behold, there were two blind men sitting by the road. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, “\f + \fr 20:30 \fq Have mercy on us, Lord, \ft ¦ Lord, have mercy on us, SBL WH ¦ Have mercy on us, Jesus, TH\f*Have mercy on us, Lord, Son of David!”
\v 31 But the crowd rebuked them, telling them to be silent. Yet they cried out even more loudly, “\f + \fr 20:31 \fq Have mercy on us, Lord, \ft ¦ Lord, have mercy on us, SBL TH WH\f*Have mercy on us, Lord, Son of David!”
\v 32 So Jesus stopped, called them over, and said, \wj “What do you want me to do for you?”\wj*
\v 33 They said to him, “Lord, let our eyes be opened.”
\v 34 Moved with compassion, Jesus touched their eyes, and immediately \f + \fr 20:34 \fq their eyes received sight, and they \ft ¦ they received their sight and CT\f*their eyes received sight, and they followed him.
\c 21
\s1 The Triumphal Entry
\m
\v 1 When they drew near to Jerusalem and came to \f + \fr 21:1 \fq Bethsphage \ft ¦ Bethphage CT TR\f*Bethsphage, at the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent two disciples ahead,
\v 2 telling them, \wj “Go into the village ahead of you, and immediately you will find a donkey tied there and a colt with it. Untie them and bring them to me.\wj*
\v 3 \wj If anyone says anything to you, say, ‘The Lord has need of them.’ And immediately he will send them.”\wj*
\v 4 Now \f + \fr 21:4 \fq all \ft ¦ — CT\f*all this took place to fulfill what the Lord had spoken through the prophet:
\b
\q1
\v 5 “Say to the daughter of Zion,
\q1 ‘Behold, yoʋr king is coming to yoʋ,
\q1 humble and mounted on a donkey,
\q1 and \f + \fr 21:5 \fq a \ft ¦ on a CT\f*a colt, the foal of a beast of burden.’ ”
\b
\m
\v 6 So the disciples went and did just as Jesus had directed them.
\v 7 They brought the donkey and the colt and laid on them their garments, and \f + \fr 21:7 \fq Jesus sat \ft ¦ they set Jesus ELZ SCR\f*Jesus sat on them.
\v 8 A very large crowd spread their garments on the road, and others cut down branches from the trees and also spread them on the road.
\v 9 The crowds that went ahead of him and those that followed were crying out,
\b
\q1 “Hosanna to the Son of David!
\q1 Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!
\q1 Hosanna in the highest!”
\b
\p
\v 10 As Jesus came into Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up, saying, “Who is this?”
\v 11 So the crowds were saying, “This is the prophet Jesus, who is from Nazareth of Galilee.” \fig The crowds that went ahead of him and those that followed were crying out, “Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest!”|src="01 Matthew 21_9.png" size="span" copy="Public Domain (Gustave Doré)" ref="21:9" \fig*
\s1 Jesus Cleanses the Temple Courts
\m
\v 12 Then Jesus entered the \f + \fr 21:12 \fq courts of God's temple \ft 96.3% ¦ temple courts CT 3.4%\f*courts of God's temple and drove out all who were selling and buying there. He overturned the tables of the money changers and the seats of those who were selling doves.
\v 13 And he said to them, \wj “It is written, ‘My house shall be called a house of prayer,’ but you\wj* \f + \fr 21:13 \fq have made \ft ¦ are making CT\f*\wj have made it a den of robbers.”\wj*
\p
\v 14 Then the \f + \fr 21:14 \fq lame and the blind \ft ¦ blind and the lame CT TR\f*lame and the blind came to him in the temple courts, and he healed them.
\v 15 But when the chief priests and the scribes saw the wonderful things that he did and the children crying out in the temple courts, “Hosanna to the Son of David,” they were indignant
\v 16 and said to him, “Do yoʋ hear what these children are saying?” Jesus said to them, \wj “Yes, have you never read,\wj*
\b
\q1 \wj ‘Out of the mouths of babies and nursing infants\wj*
\q1 \wj yoʋ have prepared praise’?”\wj*
\b
\m
\v 17 And leaving them, he went out of the city to Bethany and spent the night there.
\s1 Jesus Curses the Fig Tree
\m
\v 18 Early in the morning, as Jesus was returning to the city, he was hungry.
\v 19 Seeing a lone fig tree by the road, he came to it and found nothing on it but leaves. Then he said to it, \wj “May there never be any fruit from yoʋ again!”\wj* And at once the fig tree withered.
\v 20 When the disciples saw it, they were amazed, saying, “How did the fig tree wither all at once?”
\v 21 Jesus answered them, \wj “Truly I say to you, if you have faith and do not doubt, not only will you do what has been done to the fig tree, but even if you say to this mountain, ‘Be taken up and thrown into the sea,’ it will happen.\wj*
\v 22 \wj And if you have faith, you will receive whatever you ask for in prayer.”\wj*
\s1 The Authority of Jesus Questioned
\m
\v 23 When he entered the temple courts, the chief priests and the elders of the people came up to him as he was teaching and said, “By what authority are yoʋ doing these things? And who gave yoʋ this authority?”
\v 24 Jesus answered them, \wj “I will also ask you one question. If you tell me the answer, I will also tell you by what authority I am doing these things:\wj*
\v 25 \wj Where did John's authority to baptize come from? From heaven or from men?”\wj* So they discussed it among themselves, saying, “If we say, ‘From heaven,’ he will say to us, ‘Why then did you not believe him?’
\v 26 But we are afraid of what the crowd might do if we say, ‘From men,’ for everyone regards John as a prophet.”
\v 27 So they answered Jesus, “We do not know.” Then he said to them, \wj “Neither will I tell you by what authority I am doing these things.\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Two Sons
\m
\v 28 \wj “But what do you think? A man had two sons. He went to his first son and said, ‘Son, go work today in\wj* \f + \fr 21:28 \fq my \ft ¦ the CT\f*\wj my vineyard.’\wj*
\v 29 \wj His son answered, ‘I will\wj* \f + \fr 21:29 \fq not,’ but afterward he changed his mind and went \ft ¦ go, sir,’ but he did not go WH\f*\wj not,’ but afterward he changed his mind and went.\wj*
\v 30 \wj Then the man went to his\wj* \f + \fr 21:30 \fq other \ft ¦ second ANT RP SBL TH TR WH\f*\wj other son and said the same thing. His other son answered, ‘I will\wj* \f + \fr 21:30 \fq go, sir,’ but he did not go \ft ¦ not,’ but afterward he changed his mind and went WH\f*\wj go, sir,’ but he did not go.\wj*
\v 31 \wj Which of the two did the will of his father?”\wj* They said \f + \fr 21:31 \fq to him \ft ¦ — CT\f*to him, “The \f + \fr 21:31 \fq first \ft ¦ latter WH\f*first.” Jesus said to them, \wj “Truly I say to you, tax collectors and prostitutes are entering the kingdom of God ahead of you.\wj*
\v 32 \wj For John came to you in the way of righteousness, and you did not believe him, but tax collectors and prostitutes believed him. Yet even after you saw it, you did not change your minds and believe him.\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Vineyard Owner
\m
\v 33 \wj “Listen to another parable: There was a master of a house who planted a vineyard, put a fence around it, dug a winepress in it, built a tower, leased it to farmers, and left the country.\wj*
\v 34 \wj When the season for fruit drew near, he sent his servants to the farmers to receive his fruit.\wj*
\v 35 \wj But the farmers seized his servants; they beat one, killed another, and stoned yet another.\wj*
\v 36 \wj Again he sent other servants, more than the first group. But the farmers treated them in the same way.\wj*
\v 37 \wj Last of all, he sent them his son, saying, ‘They will have respect for my son.’\wj*
\v 38 \wj But when the farmers saw the son, they said among themselves, ‘This is the heir. Come, let us kill him and take possession of his inheritance.’\wj*
\v 39 \wj So they took him, threw him out of the vineyard, and killed him.\wj*
\v 40 \wj Now when the owner of the vineyard comes, what will he do to those farmers?”\wj*
\v 41 They said to him, “He will bring those wretches to a wretched end and lease the vineyard to other farmers who will give him the fruits in their seasons.”
\p
\v 42 Jesus said to them, \wj “Have you never read in the Scriptures,\wj*
\b
\q1 \wj ‘The stone the builders rejected\wj*
\q1 \wj has become the cornerstone;\wj*
\q1 \wj this was the Lord's doing,\wj*
\q1 \wj and it is amazing in our eyes’?\wj*
\b
\m
\v 43 \wj Therefore I tell you that the kingdom of God will be taken away from you and given to a people who will produce its fruit.\wj*
\v 44 \wj He who falls on this stone will be broken to pieces, but anyone on whom it falls will be crushed.”\wj*
\p
\v 45 When the chief priests and the Pharisees heard Jesus' parables, they knew he was speaking about them.
\v 46 Although they were looking for a way to arrest him, they were afraid of the crowds, because the people regarded him as a prophet.
\c 22
\s1 The Parable of the Wedding Feast
\m
\v 1 Once again Jesus spoke to them in parables, saying,
\v 2 \wj “The kingdom of heaven can be compared to a king who prepared a wedding feast for his son.\wj*
\v 3 \wj He sent his servants to call those who had been invited to the wedding feast, but they would not come.\wj*
\v 4 \wj Again he sent other servants, saying, ‘Tell those who are invited, “Behold, I have prepared my dinner, my oxen and the fattened cattle have been slaughtered, and everything is ready. Come to the wedding feast.” ’\wj*
\v 5 \wj But they paid no attention and went away, one to his own field and another to his business.\wj*
\v 6 \wj The rest seized his servants, mistreated them, and killed them.\wj*
\v 7 \f + \fr 22:7 \fq When the king heard about it, he \ft ¦ The king CT\f*\wj When the king heard about it, he was furious. So he sent his armies, who destroyed those murderers and burned down their city.\wj*
\v 8 \wj Then he said to his servants, ‘The wedding feast is ready, but those who were invited were not worthy.\wj*
\v 9 \wj Go therefore to where the roads exit the city and invite to the wedding feast anyone you find.’\wj*
\v 10 \wj So the servants went out to the roads and gathered together all whom they found, both evil and good, and the wedding feast was filled with guests.\wj*
\p
\v 11 \wj “But when the king came in to see the guests, he saw a man there who was not clothed with a wedding garment.\wj*
\v 12 \wj So he said to him, ‘Friend, how did yoʋ get in here without a wedding garment?’ But the man was speechless.\wj*
\v 13 \wj Then the king said to the servants, ‘Bind his\wj* \f + \fr 22:13 \fq feet and hands \ft ¦ hands and feet PCK\f*\wj feet and hands,\wj* \f + \fr 22:13 \fq take him away, \ft ¦ — NA SBL WH\f*\wj take him away, and throw him into the outer darkness. In that place there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.’\wj*
\v 14 \wj For many are called, but few are chosen.”\wj*
\s1 The Question About Paying Taxes to Caesar
\m
\v 15 Then the Pharisees went and took counsel as to how they might ensnare Jesus in what he said.
\v 16 They sent their disciples to him, along with the Herodians, who said to him, “Teacher, we know that yoʋ are true and teach the way of God in truth and defer to no one, for yoʋ do not show partiality.
\v 17 Tell us then, what do yoʋ think? Is it lawful to pay taxes to Caesar or not?”
\v 18 But Jesus knew their evil intent and said, \wj “Why are you testing me, you hypocrites?\wj*
\v 19 \wj Show me the coin for the tax.”\wj* So they brought him a denarius.
\v 20 Then he said to them, \wj “Whose image and inscription is this?”\wj*
\v 21 They said \f + \fr 22:21 \fq to him \ft ¦ — WH\f*to him, “Caesar's.” So he said to them, \wj “Then render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's.”\wj*
\v 22 When they heard this, they were amazed. Then they left him and went away.
\s1 The Sadducees Ask About the Resurrection
\m
\v 23 On that day some Sadducees \f + \fr 22:23 \fq (who say there is no resurrection) came to Jesus and \ft ¦ came to Jesus, saying there is no resurrection, and they CT\f*(who say there is no resurrection) came to Jesus and asked him,
\v 24 “Teacher, Moses said, ‘If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry the widow and raise up offspring for him.’
\v 25 Now there were seven brothers among us. The first married and died, and having no offspring, he left his wife to his brother.
\v 26 The same thing happened to the second and to the third, down to the seventh.
\v 27 Last of all, the woman \f + \fr 22:27 \fq also \ft ¦ — CT\f*also died.
\v 28 In the resurrection, therefore, which of the seven will she be the wife of? For they all had her.”
\p
\v 29 Jesus answered them, \wj “You are in error because you know neither the Scriptures nor the power of God.\wj*
\v 30 \wj For in the resurrection people neither marry nor are they given in marriage. Instead, they are like angels\wj* \f + \fr 22:30 \fq of God \ft ¦ — NA WH\f*\wj of God in heaven.\wj*
\v 31 \wj But as for the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what God said to you:\wj*
\v 32 \wj ‘I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob’? He is not the God of the dead, but of the living.”\wj*
\v 33 And when the crowds heard this, they were astonished at his teaching.
\s1 The Greatest Commandment
\m
\v 34 Now when the Pharisees heard that Jesus had silenced the Sadducees, they gathered together.
\v 35 And one of them, a lawyer, asked Jesus a question to test him:
\v 36 “Teacher, which is the greatest commandment in the law?”
\v 37 Jesus said to him, \wj “ ‘Yoʋ shall love the Lord yoʋr God with all yoʋr heart, with all yoʋr soul, and with all yoʋr mind.’\wj*
\v 38 \wj This is the\wj* \f + \fr 22:38 \fq most important and greatest \ft ¦ greatest and most important CT\f*\wj most important and greatest commandment.\wj*
\v 39 \wj And a second\wj* \f + \fr 22:39 \fq like it is this \ft ¦ is like it ANT HF NA RP SBL TR WH\f*\wj like it is this: ‘Yoʋ shall love yoʋr neighbor as yoʋrself.’\wj*
\v 40 \wj All the Law and the Prophets hang on these two commandments.”\wj*
\s1 Whose Son Is the Christ?
\m
\v 41 While the Pharisees were gathered together, Jesus asked them,
\v 42 \wj “What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is he?”\wj* They said to him, “David's.”
\v 43 He said to them, \wj “How is it then that David, in the Spirit, calls him ‘Lord,’ saying,\wj*
\b
\q1
\v 44 \wj ‘The Lord said to my Lord,\wj*
\q1 \wj “Sit at my right hand\wj*
\q1 \wj until I\wj* \f + \fr 22:44 \fq make yoʋr enemies a footstool for \ft ¦ put yoʋr enemies under CT\f*\wj make yoʋr enemies a footstool for yoʋr feet” ’?\wj*
\b
\m
\v 45 \wj If David calls him ‘Lord,’ how is he his son?”\wj*
\v 46 No one was able to say anything in reply, nor did anyone dare to ask him questions anymore from that day forward.
\c 23
\s1 Woe to the Scribes and Pharisees
\m
\v 1 Then Jesus said to the crowds and to his disciples,
\v 2 \wj “The scribes and the Pharisees sit on Moses' seat.\wj*
\v 3 \wj Therefore\wj* \f + \fr 23:3 \fq observe and do all that they tell you to observe \ft 95% ¦ do and observe all that they tell you CT 0.5%\f*\wj observe and do all that they tell you to observe. But do not do what they do, for they say things and do not do them.\wj*
\v 4 \wj They bind\wj* \f + \fr 23:4 \fq burdens that are heavy and hard to bear \ft ¦ heavy burdens SBL WH\f*\wj burdens that are heavy and hard to bear and lay them on people's shoulders, but they themselves are not willing to lift a finger to move them.\wj*
\v 5 \wj They do all their works to be seen by others.\wj* \f + \fr 23:5 \fq They \ft ¦ For they ANT CT\f*\wj They make their phylacteries wide and\wj* \f + \fr 23:5 \fq the fringes of their garments \ft ¦ their fringes CT\f*\wj the fringes of their garments long.\wj*
\v 6 \wj They love the place of honor at banquets, the best seats in the synagogues,\wj*
\v 7 \wj greetings in the marketplaces, and when others call them ‘\wj*\f + \fr 23:7 \fq Rabbi, \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj Rabbi, Rabbi.’\wj*
\v 8 \wj But you are not to be called ‘Rabbi,’ for there is one who is your\wj* \f + \fr 23:8 \fq instructor \ft ¦ teacher ANT CT PCK\f*\wj instructor,\wj* \f + \fr 23:8 \fq the Christ, \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj the Christ, and you are all brothers.\wj*
\v 9 \wj And do not call anyone on earth your father, for there is one who is your Father,\wj* \f + \fr 23:9 \fq he who is in heaven \ft ¦ the heavenly one CT\f*\wj he who is in heaven.\wj*
\v 10 \wj Neither are you to be called instructors, for there is one who is your instructor, the Christ.\wj*
\v 11 \wj But he who is greater among you must be your servant.\wj*
\v 12 \wj For whoever exalts himself will be humbled, and whoever humbles himself will be exalted.\wj*
\p
\v 13 \wj “But woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you\wj* \f + \fr 23:13 \fq devour widows' houses and for a pretense make long prayers. Therefore you will receive a more severe judgment. “Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you shut people out of the kingdom of heaven. You yourselves do not enter, nor do you let in those who are trying to enter. \ft ¦ shut people out of the kingdom of heaven. You yourselves do not enter, nor do you let in those who are trying to enter. “Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you devour widows' houses and for a pretense make long prayers. Therefore you will receive a more severe judgment. ELZ NA SCR ¦ shut people out of the kingdom of heaven. You yourselves do not enter, nor do you let in those who are trying to enter. SBL TH WH {Note: NA encloses with double brackets the words \+it Woe to you scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you devour widows' houses and for a pretense make long prayers. Therefore you will receive a more severe judgment.\+it*}\f*\wj devour widows' houses and for a pretense make long prayers. Therefore you will receive a more severe judgment.\wj*
\p
\v 14 \wj “Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you shut people out of the kingdom of heaven. You yourselves do not enter, nor do you let in those who are trying to enter.\wj*
\p
\v 15 \wj “Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you cross sea and land to make a single convert, and when he has become one, you make him twice as much a son of hell as yourselves.\wj*
\p
\v 16 \wj “Woe to you, blind guides, who say, ‘If anyone swears by the temple, it is nothing; but if anyone swears by the gold of the temple, he is bound by his oath.’\wj*
\v 17 \wj You fools and blind men! Which is greater, the gold or the temple that\wj* \f + \fr 23:17 \fq makes \ft ¦ has made CT\f*\wj makes the gold sacred?\wj*
\v 18 \wj You also say, ‘If anyone swears by the altar, it is nothing; but if anyone swears by the gift on the altar, he is bound by his oath.’\wj*
\v 19 \wj You\wj* \f + \fr 23:19 \fq fools and \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj fools and blind men! Which is greater, the gift or the altar that makes the gift sacred?\wj*
\v 20 \wj Therefore, whoever swears by the altar swears by it and by everything on it.\wj*
\v 21 \wj And whoever swears by the temple swears by it and by him who dwells in it.\wj*
\v 22 \wj And whoever swears by heaven swears by the throne of God and by him who sits upon it.\wj*
\p
\v 23 \wj “Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you tithe mint, dill, and cumin, yet you have neglected the weightier matters of the law: justice, mercy, and faith. These you ought to have practiced without neglecting the others.\wj*
\v 24 \wj You blind guides,\wj* \f + \fr 23:24 \fq who strain … swallow \ft ¦ straining … swallowing WH\f*\wj who strain out a gnat but swallow a camel!\wj*
\p
\v 25 \wj “Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you cleanse the outside of the cup and the dish, but the inside is filled with the fruits of your robbery and\wj* \f + \fr 23:25 \fq unrighteousness \ft ¦ self-indulgence CT TR\f*\wj unrighteousness.\wj*
\v 26 \wj Yoʋ blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup\wj* \f + \fr 23:26 \fq and the dish \ft ¦ — NA\f*\wj and the dish, and then the outside of\wj* \f + \fr 23:26 \fq them \ft ¦ it NA SBL WH\f*\wj them will also be clean.\wj*
\p
\v 27 \wj “Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs, which appear beautiful on the outside, but inside they are full of the bones of the dead and all uncleanness.\wj*
\v 28 \wj In the same way, you also appear righteous to others on the outside, but inside you are full of hypocrisy and lawlessness.\wj*
\p
\v 29 \wj “Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you build the tombs of the prophets and adorn the graves of the righteous,\wj*
\v 30 \wj and you say, ‘If we had been alive in the days of our fathers, we would not have been participants with them in shedding the blood of the prophets.’\wj*
\v 31 \wj Therefore you bear witness against yourselves that you are the sons of those who murdered the prophets.\wj*
\v 32 \wj Fill up, then, the measure of your fathers.\wj*
\v 33 \wj You serpents, you brood of vipers! How will you escape being sentenced to hell?\wj*
\v 34 \wj Therefore, behold, I am sending you prophets, sages, and scribes. Some of them you will kill and crucify, and some of them you will flog in your synagogues and persecute from town to town.\wj*
\v 35 \wj As a result, all the righteous blood that has been shed on earth will come upon you, from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah son of Barachiah, whom you murdered between the temple and the altar.\wj*
\v 36 \wj Truly I say to you, all these things will come upon this generation.\wj*
\s1 The Lament over Jerusalem
\m
\v 37 \wj “O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, the city that kills the prophets and stones those who are sent to yoʋ! How often I have wanted to gather yoʋr children together as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, but you were not willing!\wj*
\v 38 \wj Behold, your house is left to you\wj* \f + \fr 23:38 \fq desolate \ft ¦ — WH\f*\wj desolate.\wj*
\v 39 \wj For I tell you, from now on you will certainly not see me until you say, ‘Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!’ ”\wj*
\c 24
\s1 Jesus Predicts the Destruction of the Temple
\m
\v 1 As Jesus came out from the temple courts and was walking away, his disciples came to him to show him the buildings of the temple.
\v 2 But Jesus \f + \fr 24:2 \fq said to \ft ¦ answered CT\f*said to them, \wj “You see all these buildings? Truly I say to you, not one stone will be left here upon another; all of them will be torn down.”\wj*
\s1 Signs of the End of the Age
\m
\v 3 Later, as Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives, the disciples came to him privately and said, “Tell us, when will these things happen, and what will be the sign of yoʋr coming and of the end of the age?”
\v 4 Jesus answered them, \wj “Make sure no one leads you astray.\wj*
\v 5 \wj For many will come in my name, saying, ‘I am the Christ,’ and they will lead many astray.\wj*
\v 6 \wj You will\wj* \f + \fr 24:6 \fq hear \ft ¦ be concerned when you hear PCK\f*\wj hear of wars and rumors of wars. Do not be alarmed, for\wj* \f + \fr 24:6 \fq all \ft 92.3% ¦ — CT 1%\f*\wj all these things must take place, but the end is not yet.\wj*
\v 7 \wj For nation will rise up against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. There will be\wj* \f + \fr 24:7 \fq famines, plagues, \ft ¦ famines CT\f*\wj famines, plagues, and earthquakes in various places.\wj*
\v 8 \wj But all these things are the beginning of the labor pains.\wj*
\p
\v 9 \wj “Then they will hand you over to be tortured and will put you to death, and you will be hated by all nations because of my name.\wj*
\v 10 \wj Many will fall away and will betray one another and hate one another.\wj*
\v 11 \wj Many false prophets will rise up and lead many astray.\wj*
\v 12 \wj Because lawlessness will be increased, the love of many will grow cold,\wj*
\v 13 \wj but he who endures to the end will be saved.\wj*
\v 14 \wj And this gospel of the kingdom will be proclaimed in the whole world as a testimony to all nations, and then the end will come.\wj*
\s1 The Great Tribulation
\m
\v 15 \wj “So when you see the abomination of desolation, which was spoken of by the prophet Daniel, standing in the holy place”\wj* (let the reader understand),
\v 16 \wj “then those who are in Judea must flee to the mountains.\wj*
\v 17 \wj He who is on the housetop must not come down to get anything out of his house.\wj*
\v 18 \wj And he who is in the field must not turn back to get his\wj* \f + \fr 24:18 \fq garments \ft ¦ garment CT PCK\f*\wj garments.\wj*
\v 19 \wj Woe to those who are with child and to those who are nursing infants in those days!\wj*
\v 20 \wj Pray that your flight will not happen in winter or on a Sabbath.\wj*
\v 21 \wj For at that time there will be a great tribulation unlike any other from the beginning of the world until now, and it will never be equaled again.\wj*
\v 22 \wj If those days had not been cut short, no flesh would be saved. But for the sake of the chosen those days will be cut short.\wj*
\v 23 \wj If anyone says to you at that time, ‘Behold, here is the Christ!’ or, ‘Here he is!’ do not believe him.\wj*
\v 24 \wj For false christs and false prophets will rise up and perform great signs and wonders so\wj* \f + \fr 24:24 \fq as to lead astray, if possible, even the chosen \ft ¦ that, if possible, even the chosen might be led astray WH\f*\wj as to lead astray, if possible, even the chosen.\wj*
\v 25 \wj Behold, I have told you in advance.\wj*
\v 26 \wj So if they say to you, ‘Behold, he is in the wilderness,’ do not go out, or, ‘Behold, he is in the inner rooms,’ do not believe it.\wj*
\v 27 \wj For just as lightning comes from the east and shines to the west, so will be the coming of the Son of Man.\wj*
\v 28 \f + \fr 24:28 \fq For where \ft ¦ Where CT\f*\wj For where the carcass is, there the vultures will be gathered together.\wj*
\s1 The Coming of the Son of Man
\m
\v 29 \wj “But immediately after the tribulation of those days, the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light. The stars will fall from heaven, and the powers of the heavens will be shaken.\wj*
\v 30 \wj Then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in the heavens, and all the tribes of the earth will mourn when they see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven with power and great glory.\wj*
\v 31 \wj And he will send his angels with a loud trumpet call, and they will gather together his chosen from the four winds, from one end of the heavens to the other.\wj*
\s1 The Lesson of the Fig Tree
\m
\v 32 \wj “Now learn this lesson from the fig tree: As soon as its branches become tender and put out leaves, you know that summer is near.\wj*
\v 33 \wj So also, when you see all these things, know that he is near, at the very gates.\wj*
\v 34 \wj Truly I say to you, this generation will certainly not pass away until all these things have taken place.\wj*
\v 35 \wj Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will certainly not pass away.\wj*
\s1 Be Ready
\m
\v 36 \wj “No one knows about that day or hour, not even the angels of\wj* \f + \fr 24:36 \fq heaven, \ft 93.8% ¦ heaven, nor the Son, CT 6.1%\f*\wj heaven, but only\wj* \f + \fr 24:36 \fq my \ft ¦ the CT\f*\wj my Father.\wj*
\v 37 \f + \fr 24:37 \fq As \ft ¦ For as CT\f*\wj As were the days of Noah, so will be the coming of the Son of Man.\wj*
\v 38 \wj For in\wj* \f + \fr 24:38 \fq the \ft ¦ those NA WH\f*\wj the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, until the day when Noah went into the ark.\wj*
\v 39 \wj They knew nothing about what would happen until the flood came and took them all away. So will it be at the coming of the Son of Man.\wj*
\v 40 \wj Two men will be in the field; one will be taken and one will be left.\wj*
\v 41 \wj Two women will be grinding grain at the mill; one will be taken and one will be left.\wj*
\v 42 \wj Therefore keep watch, for you do not know\wj* \f + \fr 24:42 \fq in what hour \ft ¦ on what day CT\f*\wj in what hour your Lord is coming.\wj*
\v 43 \wj But know this: If the master of the house had known at what time of night the thief was coming, he would have kept watch and not allowed his house to be broken into.\wj*
\v 44 \wj Therefore you also must be ready, for the Son of Man is coming at an hour when you do not expect him.\wj*
\p
\v 45 \wj “Who then is the faithful and wise servant whom his master has set over his household to give them their food at the proper time?\wj*
\v 46 \wj Blessed is that servant whom his master finds doing so when he comes.\wj*
\v 47 \wj Truly I say to you, he will set him over all his possessions.\wj*
\v 48 \wj But if that evil servant says in his heart, ‘My master is delayed\wj* \f + \fr 24:48 \fq in coming \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj in coming,’\wj*
\v 49 \wj and begins to beat his fellow servants and\wj* \f + \fr 24:49 \fq to eat and drink \ft ¦ eats and drinks ANT CT\f*\wj to eat and drink with the drunkards,\wj*
\v 50 \wj the master of that servant will come on a day when he does not expect him and at an hour he does not foresee,\wj*
\v 51 \wj and will cut him in two and assign his portion to be with the hypocrites. In that place there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.\wj*
\c 25
\s1 The Parable of the Ten Virgins
\m
\v 1 \wj “At that time the kingdom of heaven will be like ten virgins who took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.\wj*
\v 2 \wj Five of them were\wj* \f + \fr 25:2 \fq wise, and five were foolish \ft ¦ foolish, and five were wise CT\f*\wj wise, and five were foolish.\wj*
\v 3 \wj When\wj* \f + \fr 25:3 \fq those who were \ft ¦ the CT\f*\wj those who were foolish took their lamps, they did not take oil with them,\wj*
\v 4 \wj but the wise took oil in their flasks along with their lamps.\wj*
\v 5 \wj When the bridegroom was delayed, they all became drowsy and slept.\wj*
\v 6 \wj But in the middle of the night there was a shout: ‘Behold, the bridegroom\wj* \f + \fr 25:6 \fq is coming \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj is coming! Come out to meet him.’\wj*
\v 7 \wj Then all the virgins rose and trimmed their lamps.\wj*
\v 8 \wj And the foolish ones said to the wise, ‘Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are going out.’\wj*
\v 9 \wj But the wise replied, ‘No, there will\wj* \f + \fr 25:9 \fq certainly \ft ¦ — ANT HF PCK RP TR\f*\wj certainly not be enough for us and for you. Instead, go to those who sell oil and buy some for yourselves.’\wj*
\v 10 \wj But while they were on their way to buy it, the bridegroom came, and those who were ready went in with him to the wedding feast. Then the door was shut.\wj*
\v 11 \wj Afterward the other virgins also came, saying, ‘Lord, Lord, open the door for us.’\wj*
\v 12 \wj But he answered, ‘Truly I say to you, I do not know you.’\wj*
\v 13 \wj Therefore keep watch, for you do not know the day or the hour\wj* \f + \fr 25:13 \fq in which the Son of Man is coming \ft 89.3% ¦ — CT 10.4%\f*\wj in which the Son of Man is coming.\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Talents
\m
\v 14 \wj “For it is just like a man about to go on a journey, who called his servants and entrusted his possessions to them.\wj*
\v 15 \wj To one he gave five talents, to another two talents, and to another one talent, each according to his ability. And he\wj* \f + \fr 25:15 \fq immediately went on his journey. The man who had received the five talents \ft ¦ went on his journey. The man who had received the five talents immediately CT\f*\wj immediately went on his journey.\wj*
\v 16 \wj The man who had received the five talents went and traded with them, and\wj* \f + \fr 25:16 \fq made \ft ¦ earned CT\f*\wj made five more\wj* \f + \fr 25:16 \fq talents \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj talents.\wj*
\v 17 \wj In the same way, the man who had received the two talents\wj* \f + \fr 25:17 \fq also \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj also earned two more.\wj*
\v 18 \wj But the man who had received the one talent went and dug a hole in the ground and hid his master's money.\wj*
\p
\v 19 \wj “After a long time the master of those servants came and settled accounts with them.\wj*
\v 20 \wj The man who had received the five talents came to him and brought the other five talents, saying, ‘Master, yoʋ entrusted me with five talents; behold, I have earned five more talents\wj* \f + \fr 25:20 \fq besides them \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj besides them.’\wj*
\v 21 \wj His master said to him, ‘Well done, good and faithful servant! Yoʋ have been faithful over a few things; I will set yoʋ over many things. Enter into the joy of yoʋr master.’\wj*
\v 22 \wj The man who had received the two talents also came to him and said, ‘Master, yoʋ entrusted me with two talents; behold, I have earned two more talents\wj* \f + \fr 25:22 \fq besides them \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj besides them.’\wj*
\v 23 \wj His master said to him, ‘Well done, good and faithful servant! Yoʋ have been faithful over a few things; I will set yoʋ over many things. Enter into the joy of yoʋr master.’\wj*
\v 24 \wj Then the man who had received the one talent came to him and said, ‘Master, I knew that yoʋ were a hard man, reaping where yoʋ did not sow and gathering where yoʋ scattered no seed.\wj*
\v 25 \wj So I was afraid and went and hid yoʋr talent in the ground. Behold, yoʋ have what is yoʋrs.’\wj*
\v 26 \wj But his master answered him, ‘Yoʋ evil and lazy servant! Yoʋ knew, did yoʋ, that I reap where I have not sown and gather where I have scattered no seed?\wj*
\v 27 \wj Then yoʋ should have deposited my money with the bankers, and when I came I would have received what is mine with interest.\wj*
\v 28 \wj Therefore take the talent from him and give it to the one who has ten talents.\wj*
\v 29 \wj For everyone who has will be given more, and he will have an abundance. But the one who does not have, even what he\wj* \f + \fr 25:29 \fq has \ft ¦ thinks he has PCK\f*\wj has will be taken away from him.\wj*
\v 30 \wj And throw that worthless servant into the outer darkness. In that place there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.’\wj*
\s1 The Final Judgment
\m
\v 31 \wj “When the Son of Man comes in his glory, and all the\wj* \f + \fr 25:31 \fq holy \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj holy angels with him, he will sit down on his throne of glory.\wj*
\v 32 \wj All the nations will be gathered before him, and he will separate them from one another as a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats.\wj*
\v 33 \wj He will set the sheep on his right and the goats on his left.\wj*
\v 34 \wj Then the King will say to those on his right, ‘Come, you who are blessed by my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world.\wj*
\v 35 \wj For I was hungry, and you gave me something to eat; I was thirsty, and you gave me something to drink; I was a stranger, and you took me in;\wj*
\v 36 \wj I was naked, and you clothed me; I was sick, and you looked after me; I was in prison, and you came to visit me.’\wj*
\v 37 \wj Then the righteous will answer him, ‘Lord, when did we see yoʋ hungry and feed yoʋ, or thirsty and give yoʋ something to drink?\wj*
\v 38 \wj When did we see yoʋ as a stranger and take yoʋ in, or naked and clothe yoʋ?\wj*
\v 39 \wj When did we see yoʋ sick or in prison and come to visit yoʋ?’\wj*
\v 40 \wj The King will answer them, ‘Truly I say to you, inasmuch as you did it to one of the least of these my brothers, you did it to me.’\wj*
\p
\v 41 \wj “Then he will say to those on his left, ‘Depart from me, you who are accursed, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels.\wj*
\v 42 \wj For I was hungry, and you did not give me anything to eat; I was thirsty, and you did not give me anything to drink;\wj*
\v 43 \wj I was a stranger, and you did not take me in; I was naked, and you did not clothe me; I was sick and in prison, and you did not look after me.’\wj*
\v 44 \wj Then they too will\wj* \f + \fr 25:44 \fq answer \ft ¦ answer him ANT TR\f*\wj answer, ‘Lord, when did we see yoʋ hungry or thirsty or as a stranger or naked or sick or in prison, and not take care of yoʋ?’\wj*
\v 45 \wj He will answer them, ‘Truly I say to you, inasmuch as you did not do it to one of the least of these, neither did you do it to me.’\wj*
\v 46 \wj And these will go away into eternal punishment, but the righteous into eternal life.”\wj*
\c 26
\s1 The Plot to Kill Jesus
\m
\v 1 When Jesus had finished saying \f + \fr 26:1 \fq all \ft ¦ — PCK\f*all these things, he said to his disciples,
\v 2 \wj “You know that after two days the Passover is coming, and the Son of Man will be delivered up to be crucified.”\wj*
\p
\v 3 Then the chief \f + \fr 26:3 \fq priests, the scribes, \ft ¦ priests CT\f*priests, the scribes, and the elders of the people were gathered together in the courtyard of the high priest, whose name was Caiaphas,
\v 4 and they plotted together to arrest Jesus secretly and kill him.
\v 5 But they said, “Not during the feast, lest there be a riot among the people.”
\s1 Jesus Anointed at Bethany
\m
\v 6 Now when Jesus was in Bethany in the house of Simon the leper,
\v 7 a woman came to him with an alabaster jar of very expensive ointment, and she poured it on his head as he reclined at the table.
\v 8 When \f + \fr 26:8 \fq his \ft ¦ the CT\f*his disciples saw it, they were indignant and said, “Why this waste?
\v 9 This \f + \fr 26:9 \fq ointment \ft ¦ — CT\f*ointment could have been sold for a large sum, and the money could have been given to the poor.”
\v 10 Aware of this, Jesus said to them, \wj “Why are you causing trouble for this woman? She has done a good deed to me.\wj*
\v 11 \wj You always have the poor with you, but you will not always have me.\wj*
\v 12 \wj She has put this ointment on my body to prepare me for burial.\wj*
\v 13 \wj Truly I say to you, wherever this gospel is preached in the whole world, what this woman has done will also be spoken of as a memorial to her.”\wj*
\s1 Judas Agrees to Betray Jesus
\m
\v 14 Then one of the twelve, the one called Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests
\v 15 and said, “What are you willing to give me if I deliver him up to you?” So they set out for him thirty pieces of silver.
\v 16 And from then on he was looking for an opportunity to betray Jesus.
\s1 The Passover with the Disciples
\m
\v 17 On the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread, the disciples came to Jesus and said \f + \fr 26:17 \fq to him \ft ¦ — CT\f*to him, “Where do yoʋ want us to make preparations for yoʋ to eat the Passover?”
\v 18 He said, \wj “Go into the city to a certain man and say to him, ‘The Teacher says, “My time is near. I am going to keep the Passover with yoʋ, along with my disciples.” ’ ”\wj*
\v 19 So the disciples did as Jesus had directed them and prepared the Passover.
\p
\v 20 When evening came, Jesus was reclining at the table with the \f + \fr 26:20 \fq twelve \ft ¦ twelve disciples SBL WH\f*twelve.
\v 21 As they were eating, he said, \wj “Truly I say to you, one of you will betray me.”\wj*
\v 22 Greatly distressed, they began to say to him one after another, “Surely not I, Lord?”
\v 23 Jesus answered, \wj “The one who has dipped his hand with me into the dish will betray me.\wj*
\v 24 \wj The Son of Man indeed goes just as it is written of him, but woe to that man by whom the Son of Man is betrayed! It would have been better for that man if he had not been born.”\wj*
\v 25 Then Judas, who betrayed him, replied, “Surely not I, Rabbi?” Jesus said to him, \wj “Yoʋ have said it yoʋrself.”\wj*
\s1 The Institution of the Lord's Supper
\m
\v 26 As they were eating, Jesus took \f + \fr 26:26 \fq the \ft ¦ — CT\f*the bread, and when he had \f + \fr 26:26 \fq given thanks \ft ¦ blessed it CT PCK TR\f*given thanks, he broke it, gave it to the disciples, and said, \wj “Take and eat this bread; this is my body.”\wj*
\v 27 Then he took \f + \fr 26:27 \fq the \ft ¦ a CT\f*the cup, and when he had given thanks, he gave it to them, saying, \wj “Drink from it, all of you,\wj*
\v 28 \wj for this is my blood of the\wj* \f + \fr 26:28 \fq new \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj new covenant, which is poured out for many for the remission of sins.\wj*
\v 29 \wj But I tell you, from this point forward I will certainly not drink again from this fruit of the vine until that day when I drink it with you anew in the kingdom of my Father.”\wj*
\v 30 And after singing a hymn, they went out to the Mount of Olives.
\s1 Jesus Predicts Peter's Denial
\m
\v 31 Then Jesus said to them, \wj “All of you will fall away because of me this night. For it is written, ‘I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered.’\wj*
\v 32 \wj But after I have been raised up, I will go ahead of you to Galilee.”\wj*
\v 33 Peter answered him, “Even if everyone else falls away because of yoʋ, I will never fall away.”
\v 34 Jesus said to him, \wj “Truly I say to yoʋ that on this night, before a rooster crows, yoʋ will deny me three times.”\wj*
\v 35 But Peter said to him, “Even if I must die with yoʋ, I will certainly not deny yoʋ.” And all the disciples said the same.
\s1 Jesus Prays in the Garden of Gethsemane
\m
\v 36 Then Jesus went with them to a place called Gethsemane, and he said to the disciples, \wj “Sit here while I go over there and pray.”\wj*
\v 37 And taking with him Peter and the two sons of Zebedee, he began to be sorrowful and distressed.
\v 38 Then Jesus said to them, \wj “My soul is very sorrowful, even to the point of death; stay here and keep watch with me.”\wj*
\v 39 And \f + \fr 26:39 \fq drawing a little closer \ft ¦ going a little farther ANT NA PCK SBL TR WH\f*drawing a little closer, he fell on his face and prayed, \wj “My Father, if possible, let this cup pass from me; nevertheless, not as I will, but as yoʋ will.”\wj*
\v 40 Then he came to the disciples and found them sleeping, and he said to Peter, \wj “So you could not keep watch with me for one hour?\wj*
\v 41 \wj Keep watch and pray, lest you enter into temptation. The spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak.”\wj*
\v 42 Again, he went away for a second time and prayed, \wj “My Father, if this\wj* \f + \fr 26:42 \fq cup \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj cup cannot pass\wj* \f + \fr 26:42 \fq from me \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj from me unless I drink it, may yoʋr will be done.”\wj*
\v 43 Then he came \f + \fr 26:43 \fq and found them sleeping again \ft ¦ again and found them sleeping CT\f*and found them sleeping again, for their eyes were heavy.
\v 44 So he left \f + \fr 26:44 \fq them and went away again \ft ¦ them again and went away CT\f*them and went away again and prayed for a third time, saying the same \f + \fr 26:44 \fq thing \ft ¦ thing again NA SBL WH\f*thing.
\v 45 Then he came to \f + \fr 26:45 \fq his \ft ¦ the CT\f*his disciples and said to them, \wj “Sleep on now and take your rest! Behold, the hour has drawn near, and the Son of Man is delivered up into the hands of sinners.\wj*
\v 46 \wj Rise, let us go. Behold, my betrayer\wj* \f + \fr 26:46 \fq has drawn near \ft ¦ is here PCK\f*\wj has drawn near.”\wj* \fig And going a little farther, he fell on his face and prayed, “My Father, if possible, let this cup pass from me; nevertheless, not as I will, but as yoʋ will.”|src="01 Matthew 26_39 (Heinrich Hofmann).png" size="span" copy="Public Domain (Heinrich Hofmann)" ref="26:39" \fig*
\s1 Jesus Arrested
\m
\v 47 While he was still speaking, behold, Judas, one of the twelve, came. With him was a large crowd with swords and clubs, sent from the chief priests and elders of the people.
\v 48 Now his betrayer had given them a sign, saying, “Whomever I kiss, he is the one; arrest him.”
\v 49 Immediately he went up to Jesus and said, “Greetings, Rabbi!” And he kissed him.
\v 50 Jesus said to him, \wj “Friend,\wj* \wj do what yoʋ have come to do.”\wj* Then they came forward, took hold of Jesus, and arrested him.
\v 51 And behold, one of the men with Jesus stretched out his hand, drew his sword, and struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
\v 52 Then Jesus said to him, \wj “Put yoʋr sword back in its place, for all who take the sword will\wj* \f + \fr 26:52 \fq die \ft ¦ perish CT TR\f*\wj die by the sword.\wj*
\v 53 \wj Or do yoʋ think that I cannot call on my\wj* \f + \fr 26:53 \fq Father right now, and he will \ft ¦ Father, and he will at once CT\f*\wj Father right now, and he will provide me with more than twelve legions of angels?\wj*
\v 54 \wj How then would the Scriptures be fulfilled that say it must happen this way?”\wj*
\p
\v 55 In that hour Jesus said to the crowds, \wj “Have you come out to arrest me with swords and clubs as you would against a robber? I sat daily\wj* \f + \fr 26:55 \fq with you, \ft ¦ — CT\f*\wj with you, teaching in the temple courts, and you did not arrest me.\wj*
\v 56 \wj But all this has taken place so that the Scriptures of the prophets would be fulfilled.”\wj* Then all the disciples left him and fled.
\s1 Jesus Faces the Sanhedrin
\m
\v 57 Now those who had arrested Jesus led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.
\v 58 But Peter was following him at a distance, coming as far as the courtyard of the high priest. After going inside, he sat with the officers to see the outcome.
\v 59 Now the chief \f + \fr 26:59 \fq priests, the elders, \ft ¦ priests CT\f*priests, the elders, and the whole Sanhedrin were seeking false testimony against Jesus, so that they could put him to death,
\v 60 but they did not find \f + \fr 26:60 \fq any. Even though many false witnesses came forward, they did not find any. \ft ¦ any, even though many false witnesses came forward. CT\f*any. Even though many false witnesses came forward, they did not find any. But at last two \f + \fr 26:60 \fq false witnesses \ft ¦ — CT\f*false witnesses came forward
\v 61 and declared, “This man said, ‘I am able to destroy the temple of God and rebuild it in three days.’ ”
\v 62 So the high priest stood up and said to him, “Do yoʋ make no answer? What are these men testifying against yoʋ?”
\v 63 But Jesus was silent. Then the high priest said to him, “I adjure yoʋ by the living God to tell us if yoʋ are the Christ, the Son of God.”
\v 64 Jesus said to him, \wj “Yoʋ have said it yoʋrself. Nevertheless I say to you, from now on you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of Power and coming on the clouds of heaven.”\wj*
\v 65 Then the high priest tore his garments and said, “He has blasphemed! What further need do we have of witnesses? Behold, you have now heard \f + \fr 26:65 \fq his \ft ¦ the CT\f*his blasphemy.
\v 66 What do you think?” They answered, “He deserves death.”
\v 67 Then they spit in his face and struck him. Others slapped him,
\v 68 saying, “Prophesy to us, yoʋ Christ! Who is it that struck yoʋ?”
\s1 Peter Denies Jesus
\m
\v 69 Now Peter was sitting outside in the courtyard, and a servant girl came to him and said, “Yoʋ also were with Jesus the Galilean.”
\v 70 But he denied it before them all, saying, “I do not know what yoʋ are talking about!”
\v 71 Then he went out to the gateway, and another servant girl saw him and said to the people there, “This man \f + \fr 26:71 \fq also \ft ¦ — CT\f*also was with Jesus of Nazareth.”
\v 72 But again he denied it with an oath: “I do not know the man.”
\v 73 After a little while, the people standing there came up and said to Peter, “Truly yoʋ also are one of them, for even yoʋr accent gives yoʋ away.”
\v 74 Then he began to invoke curses and to swear, saying, “I do not know the man.” And immediately a rooster crowed.
\v 75 Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said \f + \fr 26:75 \fq to him \ft ¦ — CT\f*to him: \wj “Before a rooster crows, yoʋ will deny me three times.”\wj* And he went out and wept bitterly.
\c 27
\s1 Jesus Handed Over to Pontius Pilate
\m
\v 1 Now when morning came, all the chief priests and the elders \f + \fr 27:1 \fq of the people \ft ¦ — PCK\f*of the people took counsel against Jesus to put him to death.
\v 2 They bound him, led him away, and handed him over to \f + \fr 27:2 \fq Pontius \ft ¦ — CT\f*Pontius Pilate, the governor.
\s1 Judas Hangs Himself
\m
\v 3 When Judas, who had betrayed him, saw that Jesus had been condemned, he regretted what he had done and returned the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and the elders,
\v 4 saying, “I have sinned by betraying \f + \fr 27:4 \fq innocent \ft ¦ righteous WH\f*innocent blood.” But they said, “What is that to us? See to it yoʋrself.”
\v 5 So he threw the pieces of silver into the temple and departed. Then he went away and hanged himself.
\v 6 The chief priests took the pieces of silver and said, “It is not lawful to put them into the temple treasury, since they are a price paid for blood.”
\v 7 So they took counsel and used the money to buy the potter's field as a burial place for foreigners.
\v 8 Therefore that field has been called the Field of Blood to this day.
\v 9 Then what was spoken by the prophet Jeremiah was fulfilled: “They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of him on whom a price had been set, on whom some of the sons of Israel had set a price,
\v 10 and they gave them for the potter's field, as the Lord directed me.”
\s1 Jesus Faces Pilate
\m
\v 11 Meanwhile, Jesus stood before the governor, and the governor asked him, “Are yoʋ the king of the Jews?” Jesus said \f + \fr 27:11 \fq to him \ft ¦ — NA SBL WH\f*to him, \wj “Yoʋ have said it yoʋrself.”\wj*
\v 12 But when he was accused by the chief priests and the elders, he gave no answer.
\v 13 Then Pilate said to him, “Do yoʋ not hear all the things they are testifying against yoʋ?”
\v 14 But he gave him no answer, not even to a single charge, so the governor was greatly amazed.
\s1 The Crowd Calls For Jesus' Crucifixion
\m
\v 15 Now at the feast the governor was accustomed to release one prisoner for the crowd, whomever they wanted.
\v 16 At that time they had a notorious prisoner named \f + \fr 27:16 \fq Barabbas \ft ¦ Jesus Barabbas NA SBL\f*Barabbas.
\v 17 So when the crowds were gathered together, Pilate said to them, “Whom do you want me to release for you? \f + \fr 27:17 \fq Barabbas \ft ¦ Jesus Barabbas NA SBL\f*Barabbas, or Jesus, who is called Christ?”
\v 18 (For he knew they had handed Jesus over out of envy.)
\v 19 While Pilate was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him this message: “Have nothing to do with that righteous man, for I have suffered a great deal today in a dream because of him.”
\v 20 But the chief priests and the elders persuaded the crowds to ask for Barabbas to be released and for Jesus to be executed.
\v 21 In response the governor said to them, “Which of the two do you want me to release for you?” They said, “Barabbas.”
\v 22 So Pilate said to them, “What then shall I do with Jesus, who is called Christ?” They all said \f + \fr 27:22 \fq to him \ft ¦ — CT\f*to him, “Let him be crucified!”
\v 23 Then the governor said, “Why? What evil has he done?” But they cried out all the more, “Let him be crucified!”
\p
\v 24 When Pilate saw that he was achieving nothing and that a riot was breaking out, he took water and washed his hands before the crowd, saying, “I am innocent of the blood of this \f + \fr 27:24 \fq righteous \ft ¦ — NA SBL WH\f*righteous man. See to it yourselves.”
\v 25 All the people answered, “His blood be on us and on our children!”
\v 26 Then he released for them Barabbas, but he scourged Jesus and handed him over to be crucified.
\s1 The Soldiers Mock Jesus
\m
\v 27 Then the governor's soldiers took Jesus with them into the governor's headquarters and gathered against him the entire cohort of soldiers.
\v 28 They stripped him, put a scarlet robe on him,
\v 29 and then twisted together a crown of thorns and put it on his head. They put a reed in his right hand, and kneeling before him, they mocked him, saying, “Hail, King of the Jews!”
\v 30 Then they spit on him, took the reed, and beat him repeatedly on his head.
\v 31 When they had finished mocking him, they stripped him of the robe and put his own garments back on him. Then they led him away to crucify him.
\s1 The Crucifixion
\m
\v 32 As they went out, they found a Cyrenian man named Simon. They pressed him into service, forcing him to carry Jesus' cross.
\v 33 When they came to a place called Golgotha (which means, “Place of the Skull”),
\v 34 they gave Jesus \f + \fr 27:34 \fq sour \ft ¦ — CT\f*sour wine to drink, mixed with gall. But when he tasted it, he refused to drink it.
\p
\v 35 After crucifying him, they divided his garments among themselves by casting \f + \fr 27:35 \fq lots. \ft ¦ lots. This was to fulfill what had been spoken by the prophet: “They divided my garments among themselves, and for my clothing they cast lots.” TR\f*lots.
\v 36 Then they sat down and kept watch over him there.
\v 37 Over his head they placed the written charge against him, which read, “This is Jesus, the King of the Jews.”
\v 38 Then two robbers were crucified with him, one on his right and one on his left.
\v 39 Those who passed by reviled him, shaking their heads
\v 40 and saying, “Yoʋ who would destroy the temple and build it in three days, save yoʋrself. If yoʋ are the Son of God, come down from the cross!”
\v 41 In the same way the chief priests \f + \fr 27:41 \fq also \ft ¦ — TH\f*also, along with the \f + \fr 27:41 \fq scribes, elders, and Pharisees \ft ¦ scribes and elders CT TR\f*scribes, elders, and Pharisees, were mocking him, saying,
\v 42 “He saved others, but he cannot save himself. \f + \fr 27:42 \fq If he is the king of Israel, \ft ¦ He is the king of Israel; CT\f*If he is the king of Israel, let him come down now from the cross, and we \f + \fr 27:42 \fq will \ft ¦ might ANT\f*will believe \f + \fr 27:42 \fq in \ft ¦ — TR\f*in him.
\v 43 He has put his trust in God; let God deliver him now if he wants him, for he said, ‘I am the Son of God.’ ”
\v 44 The robbers who were crucified with him were also reviling him in the same way.
\s1 The Death of Jesus
\m
\v 45 From the sixth hour until the ninth hour, there was darkness over the whole land.
\v 46 And about the ninth hour Jesus cried out with a loud voice, \wj “Eli, Eli,\wj* \f + \fr 27:46 \fq lima \ft ¦ lama TR ¦ lema CT\f*\wj lima sabachthani?”\wj* that is, \wj “My God, my God, why have yoʋ forsaken me?”\wj*
\v 47 When some of those standing there heard this, they said, “This man is calling for Elijah.”
\v 48 Immediately one of them ran, got a sponge, filled it with sour wine, put it on a reed, and gave it to him to drink.
\v 49 But the rest said, “Leave him alone. Let us see if Elijah comes to save \f + \fr 27:49 \fq him.” \ft 96.7% ¦ him.” Someone else took a spear and pierced his side, and out came water and blood. WH 1.1%\f*him.”
\v 50 Then Jesus cried out again with a loud voice and gave up his spirit.
\v 51 And behold, the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom. The earth was shaken, and the rocks were split.
\v 52 The tombs were opened, and the bodies of many saints who had fallen asleep were raised.
\v 53 (They came out of the tombs, and after his resurrection they went into the holy city and appeared to many.)
\v 54 Now when the centurion and those who were keeping watch with him over Jesus saw the earthquake and the things that had happened, they became very frightened and said, “Truly this man was the Son of God.”
\p
\v 55 Many women who had followed Jesus from Galilee and provided for him \f + \fr 27:55 \fq were \ft ¦ were also ANT PCK\f*were there looking on from a distance,
\v 56 among whom were Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and \f + \fr 27:56 \fq Joses \ft ¦ Joseph NA SBL WH\f*Joses, and the mother of the sons of Zebedee.
\s1 The Burial of Jesus
\m
\v 57 When it was evening, there came a rich man from Arimathea named Joseph, who was himself also a disciple of Jesus.
\v 58 He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. So Pilate commanded that the body be given to him.
\v 59 Joseph took the body, wrapped it in a clean linen cloth,
\v 60 and laid it in his own new tomb, which he had hewn in a rock. Then he rolled a large stone against the entrance of the tomb and went away.
\v 61 Mary Magdalene was there, and so was the other Mary, sitting across from the tomb.
\s1 The Guard at the Tomb
\m
\v 62 The next day (that is, after the day of Preparation), the chief priests and the Pharisees gathered together before Pilate
\v 63 and said, “Sir, we remember that while he was still alive that deceiver said, ‘After three days I will rise again.’
\v 64 Therefore command that the tomb be secured until the third day, lest \f + \fr 27:64 \fq his \ft ¦ the WH\f*his disciples come \f + \fr 27:64 \fq by night, steal him away, \ft ¦ and steal him away CT\f*by night, steal him away, and say to the people, ‘He has been raised from the dead.’ This last deception would be worse than the first.”
\v 65 Pilate said to them, “You have a guard of soldiers. Go and make it as secure as you know how.”
\v 66 So they went and secured the tomb by sealing the stone and posting the guard of soldiers.
\c 28
\s1 The Resurrection
\m
\v 1 After the Sabbath, at dawn on the first day of the week, Mary Magdalene came to see the tomb along with the other Mary.
\v 2 And behold, there was a great earthquake, for an angel of the Lord descended from heaven and came and rolled away the stone \f + \fr 28:2 \fq from the entrance \ft ¦ — CT\f*from the entrance and sat on it.
\v 3 His appearance was like lightning, and his clothing was as white as snow.
\v 4 The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like dead men.
\v 5 Then the angel said to the women, “Do not be afraid, for I know that you are looking for Jesus, who has been crucified.
\v 6 He is not here, for he is risen, just as he said. Come see the place where \f + \fr 28:6 \fq the Lord \ft ¦ he CT\f*the Lord lay.
\v 7 Now go quickly and tell his disciples that he is risen from the dead. And behold, he is going ahead of you to Galilee; there you will see him. Listen to what I have told you.”
\v 8 So they went \f + \fr 28:8 \fq out \ft ¦ away CT\f*out quickly from the tomb with fear and great joy, and ran to tell his disciples.
\v 9 \f + \fr 28:9 \fq As they went on their way to tell his disciples, \ft 81.6% ¦ And CT 11.6%\f*As they went on their way to tell his disciples, behold, Jesus met them and said, \wj “Greetings!”\wj* So they came to him, took hold of his feet, and worshiped him.
\v 10 Then Jesus said to them, \wj “Do not be afraid; go tell my brothers to go to Galilee, and there they will see me.”\wj*
\s1 The Soldiers Bribed to Lie
\m
\v 11 As they went, behold, some of the guards went into the city and brought word to the chief priests of all that had taken place.
\v 12 After the chief priests had assembled with the elders and taken counsel, they gave a large sum of money to the soldiers
\v 13 and said, “Tell people, ‘His disciples came by night and stole him away while we were sleeping.’
\v 14 If this matter is heard before the governor, we will persuade him and keep you free from concern.”
\v 15 So they took the money and did as they had been instructed. And this story is still being spread among the Jews today.
\s1 The Great Commission
\m
\v 16 Meanwhile, the eleven disciples went to Galilee, to the mountain where Jesus had directed them to go.
\v 17 When they saw him, they worshiped him, but some doubted.
\v 18 Then Jesus came and said to them, \wj “All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me.\wj*
\v 19 \f + \fr 28:19 \fq Go \ft ¦ Go therefore CT TR\f*\wj Go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit,\wj*
\v 20 \wj and teaching them to obey everything I have commanded you. And behold, I am with you always, even to the end of the age.”\wj*
\d \f + \fr 28:20 \fq Amen. \ft ¦ — CT\f*Amen.
